{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\Program Files\\\\PowerShell\\\\7\\\\pwsh.exe\" -Command \"Get-Content -Raw -LiteralPath 'C:/Users/brsth/.grok/skills/why/SKILL.md'\"","aggregated_output":"---\nname: why\ndescription: >\n  Multi-dimensional root cause analysis for AI agent failures, diagnostic errors,\n  and system observations. Applies Ishikawa fan-out across 5 dimensions\n  (mechanical/measurement/behavioral/process/environmental), then Five Whys per\n  dimension to trace each cause to its root. Every causal claim is labeled\n  [FACT]/[INFERENCE]/[UNKNOWN] with receipts ΓÇö the anti-premature-closure gate\n  that prevents acting on plausible narratives without evidence. Diagnostic mode\n  verifies the observation itself before investigating causes. Use for /why,\n  \"what went wrong\", \"what's the root cause\", \"why is X happening\", debugging\n  root causes, investigating failures, post-mortem analysis.\nmetadata:\n  short-description: \"Multi-dimensional root cause analysis with premise labeling\"\nargument-hint: \"[--diagnostic | --postmortem | --quick | --verify] <observation or failure>\"\nwhen-to-use: >\n  Use when the user invokes /why, asks \"what went wrong\", \"what's the root cause\",\n  \"why did X fail\", \"why is X happening\", \"debug the root cause\", or wants to\n  investigate why something broke or behaves unexpectedly. Also use when the\n  operator catches a diagnostic error and wants the causal chain traced to root.\nuser-invocable: true\nhost: grok\n---\n\n# /why ΓÇö multi-dimensional root cause analysis\n\n## Product rule\n\n**`/why` = why did this happen? (root cause analysis for a specific failure or observation)**\n\n| Different from | Because |\n|---|---|\n| `/aar` | `/aar` reviews a whole session for lessons. `/why` drills into one specific failure. |\n| `/tp` | `/tp` challenges framing of a current question. `/why` investigates causes of a failure. |\n| `/red-team` | `/red-team` adversarially tests a proposal. `/why` analyzes what already went wrong. |\n| `/design` | `/design` produces a design doc. `/why` produces a root cause analysis. |\n| `/go` | `/go` implements. `/why` analyzes ΓÇö it does NOT implement fixes. |\n\n## Why this skill exists\n\nSession 2026-07-25: the agent concluded \"receipt system hooks are not registered\" from evaluation summaries showing zeros. The conclusion was wrong ΓÇö hooks were already firing (31 receipt files with real data). The agent didn't check raw evidence because the narrative closed before the question formed. A linear Five Whys analysis would have found ONE cause (evaluation script bug). The actual failure had FIVE contributing dimensions. `/why` exists to prevent this class of error by forcing multi-dimensional investigation with premise labeling before any conclusion is acted on.\n\n**Source methodology:** [[multidimensional-root-cause-analysis-ai-agent-failures]] (Ishikawa fan-out adapted for AI agents) + [[problem-first-systems-decomposition]] (decompose before solving) + `/aar` Phase 4 layered root cause + `/design` Step 0.8 premise labeling.\n\n## Quick-fit screening (runs at invocation; 15-second assessment)\n\n**Good fit for `/why`:**\n- A specific failure, bug, or unexpected behavior needs root cause analysis\n- The operator caught a diagnostic error and wants the causal chain\n- Multiple symptoms suggest a shared root cause\n- A fix didn't work and the question is \"why not?\"\n\n**Better fit elsewhere:**\n- `/aar` ΓÇö task is \"review this whole session for lessons\" not \"analyze one failure\"\n- `/tp` ΓÇö task is \"challenge my framing\" not \"trace causes\"\n- `/red-team` ΓÇö task is \"stress-test this proposal\" not \"investigate what went wrong\"\n- `/design` ΓÇö task is \"design the fix\" not \"find the root cause\"\n- `/go` ΓÇö task is \"implement the fix\" not \"analyze why it broke\"\n- Just answer ΓÇö the question is explanatory (\"how does X work?\"), not investigative\n\nIf the task is clearly explanatory (no failure to investigate), surface ONE sentence:\n\n> \"This looks like an explanatory question, not a root cause investigation. Answer directly, or use `/why --diagnostic` if you suspect something is wrong.\"\n\nThis is advisory, not blocking.\n\n## Intent detection (Step 0)\n\nAuto-detect intent from phrasing. State the detected intent in the output header.\n\n| Intent | Trigger phrasing | What `/why` does |\n|--------|-----------------|------------------|\n| **Post-mortem** | \"why did X fail\", \"what went wrong\", \"root cause of\", \"post-mortem\" | Full multi-dimensional RCA (Steps 1-8) |\n| **Diagnostic** | \"why is X happening\", \"what's wrong with X\", \"debugging X\", \"investigating X\" | Verify observation FIRST (Step 0.5), then full RCA |\n| **Explanatory** | \"how does X work\", \"why does X exist\", \"explain X\" | Route to direct answer ΓÇö this is Q&A, not RCA |\n\nWhen ambiguous, default to **diagnostic** (highest risk ΓÇö premature closure causes the most damage in diagnostic mode).\n\n**Flags:**\n- `--diagnostic` forces diagnostic mode (verify-before-concluding)\n- `--postmortem` forces post-mortem mode (skip observation verification ΓÇö the failure is confirmed)\n- `--quick` skips fan-out; investigates only the most obvious dimension (for trivial single-cause bugs)\n- `--verify` spawns a fresh subagent to independently verify the analysis (maker-checker pattern)\n\n## Step 0.5: Verify the observation (diagnostic mode only ΓÇö MANDATORY)\n\n**Before investigating causes, verify the observation itself.** The observation might be wrong. This is the structural fix for the receipt-system failure: the observation (\"summaries show zeros ΓåÆ hooks not registered\") was wrong because the hooks WERE registered.\n\nFor the observation \"X is happening\":\n1. **Is X actually happening?** Check the raw evidence ΓÇö not summaries, not derived metrics, not second-hand reports. Read the actual files, run the actual commands.\n2. **Is the measurement correct?** Is the metric derived from the right source? Does it mean what we think it means?\n3. **Is the scope correct?** Are we looking at the right session, the right file, the right time window?\n\nIf the observation is wrong: **STOP.** The \"failure\" doesn't exist. Report what you found instead. Do not proceed to cause analysis for a problem that isn't real.\n\nIf the observation is correct but the interpretation is wrong (e.g., \"zeros in summaries\" is correct, but \"hooks not registered\" is the wrong interpretation): **correct the interpretation** before proceeding. State the corrected observation.\n\n**Receipt required:** `[FACT] <observation> ΓÇö receipt: <tool call that confirmed it>` or `[INFERENCE] <observation> ΓÇö not directly verified; would require: <what to check>`.\n\n## Step 1: State the problem in one sentence\n\nIf you can't state the problem in one sentence, the problem isn't clear enough to analyze. Ask one clarifying question.\n\nFormat: \"X is happening (or happened), when/where Y, resulting in Z.\"\n\nExample: \"The receipt system evaluation summaries report `hook_registration_status: not_registered`, but the hooks are actually firing (31 receipt files exist), causing the shadow evaluation to produce misleading zero metrics.\"\n\n## Step 2: Ishikawa fan-out ΓÇö investigate ALL 5 dimensions\n\nFor each dimension, ask: \"could a problem in this dimension have contributed?\" Investigate independently using tools (grep, ls, read, run). Do NOT accept narrative explanations ΓÇö verify with tool calls.\n\n| Dimension | Question to investigate | Examples for AI agent systems |\n|-----------|------------------------|-------------------------------|\n| **Mechanical** (code/tool/config) | Is the tool actually working? Is the config correct? Is there a bug in the code? | Hook not registered in JSON; script exits with error; config path wrong; API returns unexpected format; function has a logic error |\n| **Measurement** (how we observe) | Is what we're measuring actually what we think? Is the metric derived correctly? Is the observation from the right source? | Evaluation script checks wrong path; metric conflates two states (no-claims vs no-activity); counter increments on wrong event; unit mismatch |\n| **Behavioral** (model/agent reasoning) | Did the agent reason from evidence or from narrative? Did it verify before claiming? Did premature closure occur? | Narrative closed before evidence checked; claim stated as fact without receipt; confirmation bias; plausible narrative substituted for verification |\n| **Process** (workflow/skill design) | Does the process have a verification gate at the right point? Does it allow skipping? Is the gate targeting the right claim class? | No gate for mid-session diagnostic claims; receipt rule is advisory not structural; verification targets design decisions but not diagnostic claims |\n| **Environmental** (host/platform) | Is there a race condition, path mismatch, or platform-specific behavior? Are env vars available? Does discovery work the same way? | Multi-agent transcript race; env vars not exported to shell; glob discovery vs config-path check; Windows path encoding; concurrent sessions writing shared state |\n\n**For each dimension with a cause found, label it:**\n\n| Label | Meaning | Requirement |\n|-------|---------|-------------|\n| `[FACT]` | Directly verified: tool call, file read, or command output in this session confirms it | **Receipt format:** `[FACT] <claim> ΓÇö receipt: <tool call + what it confirmed>` |\n| `[INFERENCE]` | Reasoned from context but not directly verified: plausible but unconfirmed | Must note what would verify or refute it. Do NOT assert as fact. |\n| `[UNKNOWN]` | Cannot be determined from available evidence | Must note what information would resolve it |\n\n**Tool use is mandatory.** Do NOT investigate dimensions from memory or narrative. For each dimension, run at least one tool call (grep, read, ls, run command) to verify or refute the hypothesis. The Ishikawa fan-out is only as good as the evidence behind each dimension.\n\n## Step 3: Five Whys per dimension ΓÇö trace each cause to its root\n\nFor each dimension that has a cause, ask \"why\" repeatedly until:\n- The cause is **structural** (a code/config/measurement defect that can be permanently fixed), OR\n- 5 \"whys\" are exhausted (the cause is behavioral or systemic and cannot be drilled further), OR\n- The cause traces to an external constraint (platform limitation, third-party bug)\n\nFormat per dimension:\n```\nDimension: <name>\nCause: [FACT/INFERENCE/UNKNOWN] <cause> ΓÇö receipt/what-would-verify\n  Why? ΓåÆ <deeper cause>\n  Why? ΓåÆ <deeper cause>\n  Why? ΓåÆ <deepest structural cause or behavioral root>\nRoot: [STRUCTURAL|BEHAVIORAL] <root cause>\n```\n\n## Step 4: Classify each cause as structural or behavioral\n\n| Classification | Meaning | Fix type |\n|---------------|---------|----------|\n| **Structural** | Fixable in code, config, or measurement. Permanent ΓÇö doesn't decay under pressure. | Code/config change. Implement via `/go` or manual action. |\n| **Behavioral** | A property of the model's reasoning. Can only be probabilistically mitigated. | Rule, gate, receipt, or fresh-lens review. May decay under closure pressure. |\n\n**Structural fixes are prioritized.** Fix structural causes first because they're permanent. Behavioral causes need probabilistic mitigations that may not fire under pressure (as demonstrated this session ΓÇö the receipt rule existed but didn't fire).\n\n## Step 5: Competing explanations (MANDATORY ΓÇö anti-premature-closure)\n\nFor the leading root cause, name at least ONE alternative explanation. Check evidence for and against each.\n\nThis is the medical diagnosis field's \"differential diagnosis\" ΓÇö the #1 defense against premature closure (the #1 cognitive error in diagnosis). Forcing the generation of alternatives prevents locking onto the first plausible cause.\n\nFormat:\n```\nLeading cause: <cause>\nCompeting explanation 1: <alternative> ΓÇö evidence for: <X> / evidence against: <Y>\nCompeting explanation 2: <alternative> ΓÇö evidence for: <X> / evidence against: <y>\n[or: \"No competing explanation identified after investigation\" ΓÇö with the investigation cited]\n```\n\n**An empty competing-explanation slot without investigation is not acceptable.** If you genuinely can't find one after investigating, state what you checked.\n\n## Step 6: Falsifier per cause\n\nFor each cause found, ask:\n\n> \"If this cause were the ONLY cause, would fixing it prevent the failure from recurring?\"\n\n- If **yes** ΓåÆ this may be the sole root cause\n- If **no** ΓåÆ other dimensions are still active; fixing only this cause will not prevent recurrence\n\nThis step catches multi-dimensional failures where fixing one cause leaves others active.\n\n## Step 7: Fixes ΓÇö structural first, behavioral second\n\nFor each structural cause: name the specific code/config/measurement change that would fix it permanently.\n\nFor each behavioral cause: name the probabilistic mitigation (rule, gate, receipt, fresh-lens review). Note that it may not fire under pressure ΓÇö structural fixes for the same problem are preferred if available.\n\n**`/why` does NOT implement fixes.** It names them. Implementation is `/go` or manual action.\n\n## Step 8: Output ΓÇö root cause report\n\n```markdown\n## Root cause analysis: <observation>\n\n**Intent:** <post-mortem | diagnostic>\n**Problem (one sentence):** <problem statement>\n**Observation verified:** [FACT] yes/no ΓÇö receipt: <tool call>\n\n### Dimension table\n\n| Dimension | Cause | Label | Classification |\n|-----------|-------|-------|---------------|\n| Mechanical | <cause or \"none found\"> | [FACT/INFERENCE/UNKNOWN] | Structural/Behavioral/N/A |\n| Measurement | <cause or \"none found\"> | [FACT/INFERENCE/UNKNOWN] | Structural/Behavioral/N/A |\n| Behavioral | <cause or \"none found\"> | [FACT/INFERENCE/UNKNOWN] | Structural/Behavioral/N/A |\n| Process | <cause or \"none found\"> | [FACT/INFERENCE/UNKNOWN] | Structural/Behavioral/N/A |\n| Environmental | <cause or \"none found\"> | [FACT/INFERENCE/UNKNOWN] | Structural/Behavioral/N/A |\n\n### Root causes (Five Whys trace per dimension)\n\n<for each dimension with a cause: the why-chain and root classification>\n\n### Competing explanations\n\n<leading cause + ΓëÑ1 alternative with evidence for/against>\n\n### Falsifier\n\n<for each cause: would fixing it alone prevent recurrence?>\n\n### Recommended fixes (structural first)\n\n1. [STRUCTURAL] <fix> ΓÇö addresses <dimension> cause\n2. [STRUCTURAL] <fix> ΓÇö addresses <dimension> cause\n3. [BEHAVIORAL] <mitigation> ΓÇö addresses <dimension> cause (may decay under pressure)\n\n### Single-cause check\n\n<If all causes were fixed, would the failure recur? If yes, what's missing?>\n```\n\n## Variant routing\n\n| Invocation | Mode | When |\n|---|---|---|\n| `/why <observation>` | Auto-detect intent (default) | General use |\n| `/why --diagnostic <symptom>` | Diagnostic (verify observation first) | Investigating a live issue; observation may be wrong |\n| `/why --postmortem <failure>` | Post-mortem (skip observation verification) | Failure is confirmed; trace causes |\n| `/why --quick <observation>` | Single-dimension (skip fan-out) | Cause is obvious; confirm and move |\n| `/why --verify <observation>` | Full RCA + fresh subagent verification | High-stakes; need independent confirmation |\n\n## `--verify` mode (optional fresh subagent)\n\nWhen `--verify` is passed (or when the root cause is high-stakes), spawn a fresh subagent after Step 8 to independently verify the analysis. The subagent:\n\n1. Receives the root cause report + the original observation\n2. Independently investigates using tools (does NOT trust the report)\n3. Confirms or refutes each `[FACT]` claim by running its own tool calls\n4. Checks whether competing explanations were adequately explored\n5. Returns: VERIFIED (all claims checked out) / DISPUTED (specific claims wrong ΓÇö list them) / INCOMPLETE (dimensions missed ΓÇö list them)\n\nThis is the maker-checker pattern from [[premature-closure-narrative-sufficiency-external-approaches]] ΓÇö the strongest defense against premature closure.\n\n## Rules\n\n- **Fan-out before drill-down.** Always investigate all 5 dimensions before committing to a root cause. Single-cause analysis misses contributing factors.\n- **Label every claim.** `[FACT]` requires a receipt. `[INFERENCE]` must not be asserted as fact. `[UNKNOWN]` must note what would resolve it.\n- **Verify the observation before investigating causes** (diagnostic mode). The observation might be wrong. This is the structural fix for the receipt-system failure.\n- **Competing explanations are mandatory.** Even \"none identified\" requires citing what was checked.\n- **Structural fixes are prioritized over behavioral mitigations.** Structural fixes are permanent; behavioral fixes decay under pressure.\n- **Tool use is mandatory for each dimension.** Do NOT investigate from memory or narrative. Run tool calls to verify or refute.\n- **`/why` does NOT implement fixes.** It names them. Implementation is `/go` or manual action.\n- **`/why` does NOT auto-fire.** The user invokes it when they want root cause analysis.\n- **No premature closure.** If the first plausible cause feels like \"the answer,\" that's the signal to check the other 4 dimensions before committing. See [[reactive-pattern-matching-and-closure-pressure]].\n\n## Falsifier\n\n`/why` is wrong if, within 6 months:\n- The fan-out consistently finds only one real cause per failure (the other dimensions are always false positives). In that case, linear Five Whys alone is sufficient and the fan-out is overhead.\n- The premise labeling ([FACT]/[INFERENCE]/[UNKNOWN]) is consistently gamed (everything labeled [FACT] without receipts). In that case, the labeling needs mechanical enforcement.\n- The competing-explanations step is consistently performative (strawman alternatives listed to satisfy the requirement). In that case, the step needs structural enforcement (e.g., a validator that rejects competing explanations without evidence for/against).\n\n## Provenance\n\n- Ishikawa/fishbone method from manufacturing quality control (Ishikawa 1968), adapted for AI agent systems in [[multidimensional-root-cause-analysis-ai-agent-failures]] (2026-07-25)\n- Five Whys from Toyota Production System (Ohno 1988)\n- Premise labeling from `/design` Step 0.8 (2026-07-25), derived from [[plausible-narratives-substitute-for-verification]] and [[fabricated-causal-chain-receipt-required]]\n- Layered root-cause template from `/aar` Phase 4 (OBSERVED_FAILURE ΓåÆ IMMEDIATE_TRIGGER ΓåÆ PROXIMATE_CAUSE ΓåÆ CONTRIBUTING_CONDITIONS ΓåÆ SYSTEMIC_REUSABLE_CAUSE ΓåÆ COMPETING_EXPLANATION)\n- Problem-first decomposition from [[problem-first-systems-decomposition]] (2026-07-23)\n- Diagnostic-mode observation verification from the receipt-system failure (2026-07-25), where \"hooks not registered\" was an `[INFERENCE]` treated as `[FACT]`\n- Competing-explanations mandate from medical diagnosis literature ΓÇö the #1 defense against premature closure (Webster 2021, PMC8520040, 88 citations)\n- Fresh-subagent verification from `/tp` maker-checker pattern (Costa & Kallick 1993; Addy Osmani \"Loop Engineering\" 2026)\n\r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\Program Files\\\\PowerShell\\\\7\\\\pwsh.exe\" -Command \"Get-Content -Raw -LiteralPath 'C:/Users/brsth/Downloads/why-from-codex.txt'\"","aggregated_output":"Use this prompt:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nYou are improving the Grok Build `/why` skill in the live Windows environment.\r\n\r\nObjective\r\n\r\nProduce the most useful, evidence-rigorous `/why` skill for diagnosing failures in Grok Build, hooks, agents, receipts, verification gates, multi-repository workflows, and other agent-control systems.\r\n\r\nDo not blindly implement the design below. First inspect the actual current files and compare them with the Claude skills and shared RCA references. Preserve strengths that already exist and avoid adding procedural ceremony that does not improve findings.\r\n\r\nAuthoritative files to inspect\r\n\r\n1. Current Grok skill:\r\n   C:\\Users\\brsth\\.grok\\skills\\why\\SKILL.md\r\n\r\n2. Claude decision-archaeology skill:\r\n   C:\\Users\\brsth\\.claude\\plugins\\cache\\local\\cc-skills-analysis\\1.0.123\\skills\\why\\SKILL.md\r\n\r\n3. Claude RCA skill:\r\n   C:\\Users\\brsth\\.claude\\plugins\\cache\\local\\cc-skills-sdlc\\1.0.237\\skills\\rca\\SKILL.md\r\n\r\n4. Claude RCA references:\r\n   C:\\Users\\brsth\\.claude\\plugins\\cache\\local\\cc-skills-sdlc\\1.0.237\\skills\\__lib\\rca_investigation_protocol.md\r\n   C:\\Users\\brsth\\.claude\\plugins\\cache\\local\\cc-skills-sdlc\\1.0.237\\skills\\__lib\\evidence_tiers.md\r\n   C:\\Users\\brsth\\.claude\\plugins\\cache\\local\\cc-skills-sdlc\\1.0.237\\skills\\__lib\\hypothesis_scoring.md\r\n   C:\\Users\\brsth\\.claude\\plugins\\cache\\local\\cc-skills-sdlc\\1.0.237\\skills\\__lib\\rca_output_format.md\r\n\r\n5. Grok operating guidance:\r\n   C:\\Users\\brsth\\.grok\\AGENTS.md\r\n   C:\\Users\\brsth\\.grok\\docs\\file-editing-protocol.md\r\n\r\n6. Current Grok runtime documentation and active surface:\r\n   C:\\Users\\brsth\\.grok\\docs\\user-guide\\\r\n   C:\\Users\\brsth\\.grok\\active-surface.last.md\r\n\r\nAlso inspect relevant existing `/rca`, `/aar`, `/review`, `/check`, and `/close` skills if they exist. Do not assume Claude behavior works in Grok Build.\r\n\r\nResearch context\r\n\r\nA MAST-inspired review identified three useful coverage categories:\r\n\r\n- FC1: specification/context failures;\r\n- FC2: agent/component misalignment;\r\n- FC3: verification/task-completion failures.\r\n\r\nUse these as a diagnostic completeness checklist, not as universal failure probabilities. Do not encode research prevalence numbers unless independently verified and clearly scoped.\r\n\r\nRequired design\r\n\r\nPreserve the current strengths of Grok `/why`:\r\n\r\n- five-dimensional RCA fan-out;\r\n- Five Whys;\r\n- `[FACT]`, `[INFERENCE]`, and `[UNKNOWN]` labels;\r\n- competing explanations;\r\n- falsifiers;\r\n- observation verification;\r\n- structural-first reasoning;\r\n- no implementation unless explicitly authorized;\r\n- optional maker-checker verification.\r\n\r\nImprove it with the following:\r\n\r\n1. Evidence inventory before causal conclusions\r\n\r\nBefore declaring a root cause, inventory:\r\n\r\n- Mechanism: how the failure occurred;\r\n- State: what configuration/runtime/filesystem state existed;\r\n- Outcome: what was externally observed;\r\n- Authority: which registration/configuration proves the inspected code was active.\r\n\r\nIf evidence is missing, do not refuse to investigate. Mark the missing evidence, lower confidence, and state what conclusions are not yet allowed.\r\n\r\n2. First-divergence tracing\r\n\r\nBefore Five Whys, identify the earliest point where actual behavior diverged from expected behavior.\r\n\r\nDistinguish:\r\n\r\n- symptom;\r\n- first divergence;\r\n- immediate trigger;\r\n- proximate cause;\r\n- contributing conditions;\r\n- systemic/reusable cause.\r\n\r\n3. Agent-control lens\r\n\r\nFor failures involving hooks, gates, receipts, verification, agents, or multi-repository work, conditionally inspect:\r\n\r\n- contract/provenance;\r\n- mutation and verification ownership;\r\n- identity/session/repository scope;\r\n- enforcement boundary;\r\n- fail-open and fail-closed behavior;\r\n- semantic versus lexical feedback;\r\n- negative-path coverage.\r\n\r\nDo not force this entire lens on unrelated ordinary bugs.\r\n\r\n4. MAST completeness check\r\n\r\nUse FC1, FC2, and FC3 as a compact coverage check. Report which categories were examined and which remain unexamined. Do not treat the categories as proof of causality.\r\n\r\n5. Harmful feedback-loop detection\r\n\r\nAdd an explicit step asking whether the response to a failure changes the observed state, evidence, or agent behavior without satisfying the underlying invariant.\r\n\r\nDetect loops such as:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nhook blocks\r\nΓåÆ agent patches the nearest artifact\r\nΓåÆ local tests pass\r\nΓåÆ receipt remains semantically inadequate\r\nΓåÆ hook blocks again\r\n```\r\n\r\nAlso consider retry loops, stale-state loops, telemetry-missing loops, and cross-agent handoff loops. Distinguish harmful loops from legitimate bounded retries.\r\n\r\n6. Cause classification\r\n\r\nReplace the current binary structural/behavioral distinction with:\r\n\r\n- architecture/control-plane defect;\r\n- implementation/code defect;\r\n- workflow/process defect;\r\n- model-behavior limitation;\r\n- environment/runtime defect.\r\n\r\n7. Enforcement-specific negative paths\r\n\r\nFor hooks, gates, receipts, and verification systems, require consideration or testing of:\r\n\r\n- valid allow;\r\n- valid block;\r\n- stale receipt;\r\n- missing receipt;\r\n- wrong repository;\r\n- wrong session;\r\n- wrong worktree;\r\n- malformed output;\r\n- import failure;\r\n- runtime exception;\r\n- timeout;\r\n- fail-open behavior;\r\n- continuation/re-entry;\r\n- false positive and false negative paths.\r\n\r\n8. Contract-map check\r\n\r\nWhen the investigation concerns completion, verification, mutation ownership, or receipts, check whether an authoritative contract connects:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nintent\r\nΓåÆ files/scope\r\nΓåÆ mutation identity\r\nΓåÆ verification\r\nΓåÆ receipt\r\nΓåÆ completion claim\r\n```\r\n\r\nAbsence of the contract map is a candidate systemic cause, not automatically the root cause.\r\n\r\n9. Surprise and absent-evidence checks\r\n\r\nBefore finalizing, ask:\r\n\r\n- What would surprise us if the leading explanation were true?\r\n- What evidence should exist if it were true?\r\n- Is that evidence present?\r\n- What alternative explanation still fits?\r\n- What observation would distinguish the alternatives?\r\n\r\n10. Evidence and confidence discipline\r\n\r\nEvery important claim must include:\r\n\r\n- claim type;\r\n- exact source path, command, or artifact;\r\n- time/scope;\r\n- confidence;\r\n- falsifier;\r\n- permitted next action.\r\n\r\nUse evidence tiers if useful, but do not let a numeric confidence score replace causal proof. Current execution artifacts should outrank summaries, comments, and static speculation.\r\n\r\n11. Investigation/recommendation boundary\r\n\r\nThe skill may investigate and recommend, but must not implement changes unless the user explicitly authorizes implementation.\r\n\r\nDo not recommend ΓÇ£fixesΓÇ¥ for unconfirmed hypotheses. If the root cause is not established, recommend the next discriminating test instead.\r\n\r\nWhat not to add\r\n\r\nDo not:\r\n\r\n- create a decision-archaeology mode inside `/why`;\r\n- require exactly three to seven hypotheses;\r\n- require internet research for every investigation;\r\n- require durable state for every short investigation;\r\n- make all ten control dimensions mandatory;\r\n- copy Claude-specific MCP, CKS, CHS, or Serena assumptions;\r\n- claim live runtime behavior from direct hook tests alone;\r\n- treat passing tests as proof that the active runtime enforced the behavior.\r\n\r\nImplementation requirements\r\n\r\n1. Inspect the existing skill and references first.\r\n2. Produce a short design assessment identifying:\r\n   - what the current Grok skill already does well;\r\n   - concrete gaps;\r\n   - proposed changes;\r\n   - any rejected changes and why.\r\n3. Update the canonical Grok `/why` skill only.\r\n4. Preserve unrelated user changes.\r\n5. Follow the Grok file-editing protocol:\r\n   - do not full-overwrite an existing file;\r\n   - use a scoped patch;\r\n   - reread the edited sections afterward.\r\n6. Keep the skill operational and concise enough for an LLM to follow.\r\n7. Avoid duplicating rules already enforced by AGENTS.md unless the duplication is necessary at the point of use.\r\n8. If the design reveals a missing runtime capability rather than a skill problem, document that separately instead of pretending the skill can enforce it.\r\n\r\nValidation\r\n\r\nAfter editing:\r\n\r\n- reread the complete resulting `SKILL.md`;\r\n- compare it against the acceptance criteria above;\r\n- check for contradictions, duplicated instructions, unreachable branches, and excessive ceremony;\r\n- verify that diagnostic, postmortem, quick, and verification modes still have clear behavior;\r\n- confirm that no Claude-only mechanism was accidentally introduced;\r\n- run any existing skill lint or validation available;\r\n- if no automated validator exists, perform a structured manual review and report that limitation.\r\n\r\nFinal response\r\n\r\nReport:\r\n\r\n- files changed;\r\n- design decisions;\r\n- important rejected ideas;\r\n- validation performed;\r\n- remaining limitations;\r\n- whether the skill is ready for use.\r\n\r\nDo not claim the skill is ΓÇ£completeΓÇ¥ or ΓÇ£production-readyΓÇ¥ unless the final file and validation evidence support that claim.\r\n```\r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\Program Files\\\\PowerShell\\\\7\\\\pwsh.exe\" -Command \"Get-Content -Raw -LiteralPath 'P:/docs/handoffs/why-skill-enhancement-20260725/HANDOFF.md'\"","aggregated_output":"---\nthread_id: why-enhancement-20260725\nparent_handoff_path: none\ncurrent_session_id: 019f96f5-dc4a-79d0-9e17-396f2a582186\ncurrent_terminal_id: console_9f93f0d3-0b5b-4985-b779-6a2c\nproduced_at: 2026-07-25T18:30:00Z\nstatus: open\nhandoff_type: investigation\naccurate_as_of_head: unknown\n---\n\n# Handoff: `/why` skill enhancement ΓÇö evidence-gated causal investigation\n\n## Objective\n\nEnhance `~/.grok/skills/why/SKILL.md` from its current 5-dimension Ishikawa RCA into an evidence-gated investigation protocol with conditional control-system analysis, feedback-loop detection, and architecture/code classification. The enhancement was designed through multi-model analysis during session 019f96f5, where the current `/why` was tested against a real failure (the session's own recurring gaps) and found weaker than two external LLM analyses.\n\n## Why this matters\n\nThe current `/why` produced an analysis that was correct about individual causes but wrong about the frame. When two external LLMs analyzed the same failure pattern, both produced deeper, more architectural diagnoses. The gap is not analytical vocabulary ΓÇö it's evidence discipline, coverage completeness, and failure-pattern recognition.\n\n## Evidence for the gap\n\nSession 019f96f5 ran `/why` on \"why do I keep making gaps.\" The analysis found 5 causes across 5 dimensions. Two external analyses found 10 causes each. Key causes the current `/why` missed entirely:\n\n- No authoritative contract map connecting intent ΓåÆ files ΓåÆ identity ΓåÆ mutations ΓåÆ verification ΓåÆ completion\n- Mutation ownership and verification ownership are separate systems with no shared identity contract\n- Fail-open behavior hides infrastructure failures (ImportError ΓåÆ silent None, not ENFORCEMENT_UNAVAILABLE)\n- The recurring cycle: hook blocks ΓåÆ patch nearest artifact ΓåÆ test green ΓåÆ receipt inadequate ΓåÆ hook blocks again\n- No negative-path testing (import crash, stale receipt, identity ambiguity)\n\nThe MAST paper (Cemri et al. 2025, arxiv 2503.13657, 523 citations) independently confirms: multi-agent LLM systems fail in 14 modes across 3 categories (specification issues, inter-agent misalignment, task verification). The current Ishikawa dimensions don't cover inter-agent misalignment at all.\n\n## What to implement (approved design)\n\nThe design went through three rounds of review (my initial proposal, two external LLM analyses, operator-approved corrections). Final approved direction:\n\n### Step ordering (operator-approved)\n\n1. **Verify and define the symptom** (existing Step 0.5, unchanged)\n2. **Evidence inventory** (NEW ΓÇö replaces proposed \"evidence gate\") ΓÇö inventory present/missing/still-required/conclusions-allowed across Mechanism, State, Outcome, Authority. NOT a blocking gate ΓÇö missing evidence may itself be the finding. Proceed with explicitly provisional conclusions.\n3. **Identify the first divergence** (NEW) ΓÇö where did the system first deviate from expected behavior? Prevents jumping from symptom to architectural explanation.\n4. **MAST/control-system coverage check** (NEW) ΓÇö compact checklist using FC1 (specification), FC2 (inter-agent misalignment), FC3 (verification) as \"did you check this category?\" NOT prevalence claims. Plus a control-system lens (contract/provenance, identity/ownership, enforcement boundary, fail-open patterns, negative-path coverage) that expands only when the failure involves agent-control infrastructure.\n5. **Five RCA dimensions** (existing Step 2, unchanged) ΓÇö Mechanical, Measurement, Behavioral, Process, Environmental\n6. **Classify each cause** (Step 4 revision) ΓÇö five-way: architecture/control-plane, implementation/code, workflow/process, model behavior, environment/runtime. Replaces current binary structural/behavioral.\n7. **Detect harmful feedback loops** (NEW Step 7) ΓÇö general question: \"Does the response to the failure change the observed state, evidence, or agent behavior without satisfying the underlying invariant?\" Distinguish from legitimate retry/continuation.\n8. **Competing-explanation, surprise, absent-evidence, and falsifier checks** (existing Step 5, enhanced) ΓÇö add: \"What evidence should exist if this explanation were true? Is it present?\"\n9. **Recommend fixes** (existing Step 7, refined) ΓÇö only for sufficiently supported causes\n\n### Design decisions and objections (the \"why we chose what we did\")\n\n**Decision 1: Five dimensions + cross-cutting lens, not ten equal dimensions.**\n- My initial proposal: expand to 10 dimensions (add Contract/Provenance, Identity/Ownership, Enforcement Boundary, Negative-path Coverage, Fail-Open Patterns).\n- Objection (external LLM 2): \"expanding to ten mandatory dimensions would probably create checklist fatigue and shallow analysis.\"\n- Resolution: keep 5 dimensions, add a cross-cutting control-system lens that expands only when relevant. The lens is conditional, not mandatory for every investigation.\n- Why: the 5 existing dimensions are sound for general debugging. The 5 new agent-control dimensions are only relevant for hooks/gates/receipts/verification failures. Forcing all 10 every time wastes effort on ordinary bugs.\n\n**Decision 2: Evidence inventory, not evidence gate.**\n- My initial proposal: \"evidence-completeness gate\" requiring all four evidence types (Mechanism, State, Outcome, Authority) before investigation begins.\n- Objection (external LLM 2): \"A missing authority artifact may itself be the finding. Make the gate an evidence inventory... Otherwise `/why` may refuse to investigate precisely when authority or runtime evidence is missing.\"\n- Resolution: inventory approach ΓÇö present, missing, still-required, conclusions-currently-allowed. Proceed with explicitly provisional conclusions when evidence is incomplete.\n- Why: a blocking gate would prevent investigation exactly when the missing artifact IS the root cause. The inventory surfaces the gap without blocking.\n\n**Decision 3: Five-way classification, not binary structural/behavioral.**\n- My initial proposal listed four categories (architecture, code, behavior, environment) but called it \"five-way\" ΓÇö internally inconsistent.\n- Objection (external LLM 2): \"It says five-way but lists four... also drops process/workflow.\" Corrected list: architecture/control-plane, implementation/code, workflow/process, model behavior, environment/runtime.\n- Resolution: five-way classification as corrected.\n- Why: the current binary conflates \"one-file code bug\" with \"agent-control system design gap.\" The distinction matters because the fixes have completely different cost/risk profiles.\n\n**Decision 4: Broad feedback-loop detection, not narrow \"fix alters evidence\" formulation.**\n- My initial proposal: \"Does the fix alter the evidence used by the gate, without changing the underlying state the gate is intended to measure?\"\n- Objection (external LLM 2): \"Feedback-loop detection should be broader... other harmful loops exist\" ΓÇö identified three additional loop patterns (blockΓåÆpatchΓåÆverifyΓåÆblock, retryΓåÆpartial changeΓåÆchanged hypothesisΓåÆretry, failureΓåÆno telemetryΓåÆassume no failureΓåÆrepeat).\n- Resolution: general question \"Does the response to the failure change the observed state, evidence, or agent behavior without satisfying the underlying invariant?\" Plus distinguishing harmful loops from legitimate retry/continuation.\n- Why: the narrow formulation only catches one loop pattern. The general formulation catches all four observed patterns.\n\n**Decision 5: MAST as coverage taxonomy, not prevalence claim.**\n- My initial proposal cited MAST prevalence figures (FC1: 41.77%, FC2: 36.94%, FC3: 21.30%).\n- Objection (external LLM 2): \"Use FC1/FC2/FC3 as a coverage taxonomy, not as a claim that those categories have the same prevalence in Grok Build.\"\n- Resolution: use as \"did you check this category?\" not \"this category has this probability.\"\n- Why: MAST's dataset (7 MAS frameworks, 200 traces) may not match Grok Build's system model.\n\n**Decision 6: Contract-map check is conditional, not automatic root cause.**\n- My initial proposal: Step 0.7 checks for a contract map and if none exists, \"that's the root cause.\"\n- Objection (external LLM 2): \"'No contract map exists' should not automatically be declared the root cause... The correct output should be: 'No authoritative contract map was found. This is a candidate systemic cause.'\"\n- Resolution: report as candidate systemic cause, test whether the failure could occur if such a map existed and was enforced.\n- Why: absence of an artifact is evidence, not proof of causation.\n\n**Decision 7: Decision-archaeology mode excluded.**\n- External LLM 1 proposed adding `decision-archaeology` mode (why does this configuration exist?).\n- My objection: scope creep. `/why` is for failure analysis. Decision archaeology should be its own skill or `/wiki query` variant.\n- Operator agreed: \"keep decision archaeology out of /why.\"\n\n**Decision 8: No mandatory 3-7 competing hypotheses.**\n- External LLM 1 proposed requiring 3-7 competing hypotheses.\n- My objection: the existing \"ΓëÑ1 competing explanation\" requirement is already hard to satisfy honestly. Forcing 3-7 incentivizes strawman padding.\n- Resolution: keep ΓëÑ1, use MAST categories as a coverage checklist instead of a count threshold.\n\n**Decision 9: Durable investigation state is conditional.**\n- External LLM 1 proposed writing a compact RCA state/report artifact for every investigation.\n- My objection: adds ceremony for the 90% of cases that complete in one turn.\n- Resolution: make conditional on `--verify` mode or long investigations only.\n\n**Decision 10: Negative-path checks mandatory only for enforcement work.**\n- Both external analyses agreed: mandatory for hooks/gates/receipts/verification systems, conditional for ordinary debugging.\n\n### Additional high-ROI optimizations (post-handoff review ΓÇö 2026-07-25)\n\nThese emerged from reviewing the handoff against the Codex prompt\n(`C:\\Users\\brsth\\Downloads\\why-from-codex.txt`) and the Claude RCA reference\nat `cc-skills-sdlc/.../__lib/evidence_tiers.md`. They are NOT covered by the\n10 decisions above and have the highest long-term ROI.\n\n**Optimization A: Evidence-tier system (from Claude `evidence_tiers.md`).**\n- The handoff's \"evidence inventory\" (Mechanism/State/Outcome/Authority) is\n  necessary but not sufficient. The Claude reference adds a **Tier 1-4\n  confidence ceiling** system:\n  - Tier 1 (95%): execution artifacts, logs, test output, telemetry\n  - Tier 2 (85%): official docs, specs, peer-reviewed reference\n  - Tier 3 (75%): static analysis, logical derivation, symbols\n  - Tier 4 (50%): comments, unverified claims, speculation\n- **Rule:** overall confidence cannot exceed the weakest tier in the causal\n  chain (weakest-link). This is mechanically enforceable and prevents the\n  \"I read the code, therefore FACT\" error ΓÇö reading code is Tier 3, not Tier 1.\n- **Why high-ROI:** this single rule structurally prevents the receipt-system\n  failure class (treating an inference as a fact). It's the missing enforcement\n  layer behind the existing `[FACT]/[INFERENCE]/[UNKNOWN]` labels.\n\n**Optimization B: Pattern-library integration (RADICAL ΓÇö highest ROI).**\n- `/why` currently investigates every failure as novel. This workspace has\n  27+ wiki concepts, many documenting recurring failure patterns (closure\n  pressure, narrative sufficiency, reactive pattern matching, fail-open,\n  etc.).\n- **Add Step 0: pattern match.** Before investigating, query the wiki\n  (`/wiki query <failure-shape>`) for similar past failures. If a known\n  pattern matches, START from the known root cause and verify/disconfirm,\n  rather than re-deriving from scratch.\n- **Why highest ROI:** turns `/why` from a one-shot analyzer into a\n  cumulative-knowledge system. Each investigation builds on prior ones.\n  Directly addresses the \"external LLMs found deeper causes\" gap ΓÇö the\n  external LLMs had broader pattern libraries; our `/why` doesn't query its\n  own.\n- **Depends on:** Optimization C (the feedback loop that populates the library).\n\n**Optimization C: Feedback-to-wiki loop (RADICAL ΓÇö enables B).**\n- Every `/why` that finds a **systemic or architectural** cause should\n  auto-write (with operator confirm) a wiki concept capturing the pattern.\n- Without this, `/why` findings evaporate into session transcripts and never\n  feed back into future investigations.\n- **Threshold:** only systemic/architectural causes (not one-off code bugs).\n  The pattern must be reusable to warrant a concept.\n- This closes the loop: `/why` ΓåÆ wiki concept ΓåÆ future `/why` Step 0 query.\n\n**Optimization D: Failure-class dispatch (radical refactor of step structure).**\n- The handoff keeps a linear 8-step structure with inline \"conditional\n  expansion.\" A cleaner radical refactor: **dispatch at Step 0 based on\n  failure class**, making the conditionality concrete:\n  - `--bug`: ordinary code bug ΓåÆ mechanical dimension primary, skip\n    agent-control lens, 60-second fast path\n  - `--agent`: agent-control system (hooks/gates/receipts/verification) ΓåÆ\n    full protocol: contract-map, feedback loops, negative paths, semantic vs\n    lexical feedback\n  - `--pattern`: recurring behavioral pattern ΓåÆ wiki-first (Optimization B),\n    longitudinal across sessions\n  - `--system`: architectural/systemic ΓåÆ contract-map first, then dimensions\n- **Why:** makes \"conditional expansion\" dispatch-driven (clear modes) rather\n  than a series of inline conditionals the LLM must track. Saves effort on\n  simple bugs; guarantees depth on complex ones.\n\n**Optimization E: Six-layer first-divergence model (from Codex prompt).**\n- The handoff has \"identify the first divergence\" but the Codex prompt\n  distinguishes six layers that should not be conflated:\n  symptom ΓåÆ first divergence ΓåÆ immediate trigger ΓåÆ proximate cause ΓåÆ\n  contributing conditions ΓåÆ systemic/reusable cause.\n- **Why:** prevents the common error of conflating trigger with cause\n  (\"the hook blocked\" is a trigger; \"the receipt was semantically\n  inadequate\" is the cause). Explicit layer separation forces the\n  distinction.\n\n**Optimization F: Semantic vs lexical feedback (agent-control lens addition).**\n- For agent-control systems, distinguish \"the gate fired correctly (lexical ΓÇö\n  exit code, file written)\" from \"the gate measured the right thing\n  (semantic ΓÇö does the receipt actually prove completion?).\"\n- The receipt-system failure was lexical-correct (hooks fired, files written)\n  but semantic-wrong (receipts didn't measure test quality).\n- Add as a named sub-check within the agent-control lens (Decision 1).\n\n**Recommended implementation order:** C ΓåÆ B ΓåÆ A ΓåÆ D ΓåÆ E ΓåÆ F.\nC and B are the radical refactor (cumulative knowledge system). A is the\nenforcement layer. D is structural clarity. E and F are refinements.\n\n## What NOT to change\n\nThese existing features are sound and should be preserved:\n- Five Whys per dimension (correct drill-down method)\n- Competing-explanations requirement (correct anti-premature-closure defense)\n- Premise labeling ([FACT]/[INFERENCE]/[UNKNOWN])\n- Falsifier step\n- \"Verify observation before investigating causes\" (diagnostic mode)\n- `/why` does NOT implement fixes (implementation is `/go`)\n\n## File to edit\n\n- `C:\\Users\\brsth\\.grok\\skills\\why\\SKILL.md` (single file, ~400 lines currently)\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n1. The enhanced `/why` run on this session's failure pattern produces findings at least as complete as the external LLM analyses (10 causes, feedback-loop detection, architecture/code classification)\n2. Evidence inventory step surfaces missing authority/state evidence without blocking investigation\n3. Feedback-loop detection identifies the blockΓåÆpatchΓåÆgreenΓåÆinadequate cycle\n4. MAST categories used as coverage checklist, not prevalence claims\n5. Five-way classification distinguishes architecture from code from process from behavior from environment\n6. The skill does not become a 50-step questionnaire ΓÇö conditional expansion keeps ordinary-bug investigations short\n7. All existing features preserved (Five Whys, competing explanations, premise labeling, falsifier, verify-before-conclude)\n\n## Test case\n\nRun the enhanced `/why` on: \"Throughout session 019f96f5, I repeatedly produced incomplete work, claimed it was complete, and when caught I patched reactively.\" The output should find:\n- The feedback loop (hook ΓåÆ patch ΓåÆ green ΓåÆ inadequate ΓåÆ hook)\n- The contract-map gap (no authoritative source connecting intent ΓåÆ files ΓåÆ mutations ΓåÆ verification ΓåÆ completion)\n- The fail-open pattern (ImportError ΓåÆ silent None)\n- The enforcement-boundary gap (Stop hook measures exit code, not test quality)\n- Classification as architecture/control-plane (not just \"behavioral\")\n\n## Related wiki concepts\n\n- `P:/.data/wiki/concepts/multidimensional-root-cause-analysis-ai-agent-failures.md` ΓÇö the existing Ishikawa adaptation that this enhancement extends\n- `P:/.data/wiki/concepts/reactive-pattern-matching-and-closure-pressure.md` ΓÇö the behavioral pattern this skill is designed to diagnose\n\n## Reference material\n\n- MAST paper: https://arxiv.org/html/2503.13657v2 (Cemri et al. 2025, 523 citations) ΓÇö empirically grounded MAS failure taxonomy, 14 modes in 3 categories\n- External LLM analysis 1: \"Completion-Provenance Drift\" diagnosis (10 causes)\n- External LLM analysis 2: prioritized implementation direction with corrections\n- Session transcript: `~/.grok/sessions/P%3A%5C/019f96f5-dc4a-79d0-9e17-396f2a582186/`\n\n## Dependencies\n\n- **Requires:** nothing ΓÇö can start immediately\n- **Blocks:** nothing\n- **Non-blocking to:** other skill improvements\n\n## Status\n\nOPEN ΓÇö ready for implementation in a fresh session\n\n## Next steps\n\n1. Read this handoff\n2. Read the current `~/.grok/skills/why/SKILL.md`\n3. Read `P:/.data/wiki/concepts/multidimensional-root-cause-analysis-ai-agent-failures.md`\n4. Implement the 9-step protocol with conditional expansion per the design decisions above\n5. Test against the session 019f96f5 failure pattern (acceptance criteria above)\n6. Run `/check` to verify the skill change\n\n## Last user message (verbatim)\n\n\"/handoff for the '/why' enhancement. include the objections and decisions for why we chose what we did.\"\n\r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
