{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\Program Files\\\\PowerShell\\\\7\\\\pwsh.exe\" -Command \"Get-Content -Raw -LiteralPath 'C:/Users/brsth/.grok/skills/tp/SKILL.md'\"","aggregated_output":"---\nname: tp\ndescription: >\n  Critical-friend / thought-partner. Default /tp is a two-lens critique: a\n  fresh subagent generates the critique (different lens, no shared framing\n  anchor), then the same agent verifies and integrates each finding against\n  session evidence (verification + novelty + integration checks). Same-agent\n  dialogue survives as /tp quick for speed-over-rigor cases. Variant routing:\n  /tp (two-lens critique), /tp quick (same-agent dialogue), /tp check\n  (diagnostic scoring against failure modes), /tp session (session-end\n  opportunity review with NOW/NEXT/LATER), /tp help (intent table + matrix),\n  /tp load (deep operating-manual reference), /tp <mode> (targeted correction).\nmetadata:\n  short-description: \"Two-lens critical-friend critique with verification\"\nargument-hint: \"[quick | check | session | help | load | <failure-mode>] | [<artifact-path>] [horizon=now|next|later] [confidence=high|medium|low]\"\nwhen-to-use: >\n  Use when the user invokes /tp, asks for thought-partner dialogue, critical\n  friend review, \"help me think,\" \"challenge this,\" \"fresh eyes on this,\" or\n  wants a recommendation/verdict on a decision, proposal, design, or direction.\nuser-invocable: true\nhost: grok\n---\n\n# /tp ΓÇö two-lens critical-friend critique\n\n## Core insight\n\n**You cannot refocus your own glasses.** Costa & Kallick (1993), who\noriginated the \"critical friend\" concept, built it on this premise: a\nself-applied critique thinks harder through the same lens, which is\nstructurally weaker than a critique from a different lens.\n\n`/tp`'s default mode spawns a **fresh subagent** (no shared framing anchor)\nto generate the critique, then has the same agent **verify and integrate**\nthe findings against the session's actual evidence. Same-agent dialogue\nsurvives as `/tp quick` for speed-over-rigor cases.\n\n## The 4-dimensional routing matrix\n\nEvery `/tp` invocation is a point in a 4-dimensional space. The semantic\nclassifier picks the point automatically; explicit invocation overrides it.\n\n```\nQuestion arrives\n  Γöé\n  Γö£ΓöÇΓöÇ DIM 1: LENS (who thinks?)\n  Γöé     Γö£ΓöÇΓöÇ two-lens (spawn subagent)     ΓåÆ default /tp\n  Γöé     Γö£ΓöÇΓöÇ same-agent (inline)           ΓåÆ /tp quick, /tp check, /tp session\n  Γöé     ΓööΓöÇΓöÇ hybrid (inline + workspace)   ΓåÆ opportunity scan\n  Γöé\n  Γö£ΓöÇΓöÇ DIM 2: HORIZON (when?)        ΓåÉ auto-detected from phrasing\n  Γöé     Γö£ΓöÇΓöÇ now   ΓåÆ skip pre-mortem, steelman, second-order\n  Γöé     Γö£ΓöÇΓöÇ next  ΓåÆ include second-order\n  Γöé     Γö£ΓöÇΓöÇ later ΓåÆ full depth (all domains)\n  Γöé     ΓööΓöÇΓöÇ all   ΓåÆ default (full depth)\n  Γöé\n  Γö£ΓöÇΓöÇ DIM 3: TARGET (what?)\n  Γöé     Γö£ΓöÇΓöÇ live question      ΓåÆ default\n  Γöé     Γö£ΓöÇΓöÇ prior turn         ΓåÆ /tp check\n  Γöé     Γö£ΓöÇΓöÇ session state      ΓåÆ /tp session\n  Γöé     Γö£ΓöÇΓöÇ file/artifact      ΓåÆ /tp <path>\n  Γöé     ΓööΓöÇΓöÇ workspace state    ΓåÆ opportunity scan (subagent scans)\n  Γöé\n  ΓööΓöÇΓöÇ DIM 4: POSTURE (how?)\n        Γö£ΓöÇΓöÇ critique           ΓåÆ default /tp\n        Γö£ΓöÇΓöÇ diagnostic         ΓåÆ /tp check\n        Γö£ΓöÇΓöÇ opportunity review ΓåÆ /tp session\n        ΓööΓöÇΓöÇ dialogue           ΓåÆ /tp quick\n```\n\n**The matrix is a mental model, not an executable routing table.** It\norganizes how the skill thinks about invocation space; the actual routing\nis the screening logic in the sections below (semantic intent\nclassification, quick-fit screening, inline carve-out, variant table).\nDo not treat the matrix as if a future session will mechanically\ndispatch on it ΓÇö the classifier and the variant table do the dispatching;\nthe matrix explains why they're shaped the way they are.\n\n### Confidence dimension (adapts depth to uncertainty)\n\nNew (from `/reason` routing table research). Confidence calibrates how\nmany critique domains fire. It does not replace horizon ΓÇö it composes\nwith it (whichever demands more depth wins).\n\n| Confidence | Signal | Depth effect |\n|---|---|---|\n| **high** | The answer feels settled; the user wants validation, not excavation | Core domains only (framing, optimal, falsifiability, anchoring). Skip pre-mortem, steelman, second-order, solution-space broadening. |\n| **medium** (default) | Normal critique ΓÇö the framing might be off, alternatives might exist | Core domains + solution-space broadening. Skip pre-mortem, steelman, second-order unless horizonΓëánow. |\n| **low** | Something feels off; the answer seems too neat; high stakes | Full depth: all core + pre-mortem + steelman + second-order + solution-space. |\n\n**Auto-detection:** infer confidence from the question's hedging. \"Is this\nright?\" ΓåÆ medium. \"This feels too easy\" / \"what am I missing?\" ΓåÆ low.\n\"Quick sanity check\" ΓåÆ high. State the inferred confidence in the synthesis\nheader. Explicit `confidence=low` overrides.\n\n**Composes with horizon (mechanical rule):** depth = `max(horizon_depth, confidence_depth)`. Compute each dimension's depth independently, then run the union.\n\n| horizon | horizon_depth | confidence | confidence_depth | runs |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| now | core 1-4 + 5 | high | core 1-4 | core 1-4 + 5 |\n| now | core 1-4 + 5 | medium | core 1-4 + 5 | core 1-4 + 5 |\n| now | core 1-4 + 5 | low | all | all |\n| later | all | high | core 1-4 | all (horizon wins) |\n| later | all | medium | core 1-4 + 5 | all (horizon wins) |\n| later | all | low | all | all |\n\n\"all\" = core 1-5 + second-order (2a) + pre-mortem (3a) + steelman (4a). When either dimension demands a domain, it runs. No domain is dropped because the other dimension is permissive.\n\n## Semantic intent classification (replaces trigger-phrase matching)\n\nInstead of matching specific phrases, classify the user's intent at\nscreening time. This adapts to new phrasings naturally ΓÇö the model reads\nthe question and determines intent.\n\n| Intent | Signal | Routing |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Session review** | User asks what matters, what to care about, what they might be missing FROM THIS SESSION | `/tp session` protocol (NOW/NEXT/LATER/FILTER). Always inline (session-state). |\n| **Opportunity scan (hybrid)** | User asks about open work, outstanding items, continuation candidates | Hybrid: session state (bundle) + workspace state (subagent scans handoffs, plans, tasks). Spawn with workspace-scan scope. |\n| **Session close check** | User asks whether the session is closeable or asks for a summary | Inline. Run `/close` instead if gates haven't been checked. |\n| **Critique** | User asks whether something is good/right/optimal, wants a challenge, fresh eyes | Default two-lens `/tp`. |\n\n**Explicit invocation** (`/tp session`, `/tp check`, `/tp quick`) bypasses\nclassification and runs the named variant directly.\n\n## Quick-fit screening (runs at invocation; 30-second assessment)\n\n**Good fit for /tp:** multiple valid answers needing framing challenge; the\nframing itself might be wrong; critical-friend lens is the right posture.\n\n**Better fit elsewhere:**\n- `/brainstorming` ΓÇö \"I have an idea for a new feature/project\" needing structured design exploration with one-at-a-time clarifying questions and a written spec. (/tp handles solution-space broadening itself via core domain 5.)\n- `/plan` ΓÇö \"triage these N options\" (multi-decision triage)\n- `/design` ΓÇö \"produce a design doc for X\"\n- `/go` ΓÇö \"implement/fix/build\"\n- Just answer ΓÇö single factual question with a well-known answer\n\n**Inline carve-out (session-state questions):** if the question depends\nENTIRELY on current session state (conversation history, tool calls,\ndecisions), run inline ΓÇö the subagent cannot reconstruct conversation\nstate from files. **Exception ΓÇö hybrid questions** (\"what are we\nforgetting?\", \"what's still open?\") need BOTH session state AND workspace\nstate; for these, spawn with workspace-scan scope (grep handoffs, read\nplans, check task stores) and provide the session-state portion in the\ncontext bundle. Reference incident (2026-07-23): two `/tp` spawns for\nsession-close questions were cancelled; one ran 188.7s / 14 tool calls\nbefore cancellation ΓÇö all wasted because the question depended on session\nstate the subagent didn't have.\n\n**Inline disclosure** (use INSTEAD of Step 4 fallback disclosure ΓÇö this\nis structurally appropriate, not a degradation):\n> ΓÜá∩╕Å **INLINE (structurally appropriate):** This question depends on\n> session state. The fresh subagent cannot reconstruct session state from\n> files, so the two-lens architecture collapses. Running inline is correct.\n\n**Solution-space broadening is built in.** /tp does NOT route away when\nthe question seems under-specified. Core domain 5 (\"What else is\npossible?\") ensures every critique surfaces alternatives. If the task is\nclearly a poor fit, surface ONE sentence before spawning:\n> \"This task looks like a better fit for `/<other-skill>` because <reason>. Proceed with `/tp` anyway?\"\n(Advisory, not blocking.)\n\n## Variant routing\n\n| Invocation | Lens | Action |\n|---|---|---|\n| `/tp` | Two-lens (fresh + synthesis) | **Default.** Fresh subagent critique + same-agent verification. ~60-90s. |\n| `/tp quick` | Same-agent | Fast dialogue. User accepts same-lens limitation. |\n| `/tp check` | Same-agent | Diagnostic scoring against failure modes. Operates on prior turn. |\n| `/tp <failure-mode>` | Same-agent | Targeted correction (e.g. `/tp agreeableness`). |\n| `/tp session` | Same-agent (inline) | NOW/NEXT/LATER/FILTER opportunity review. Invoked by `/close`. |\n| `/tp help` | ΓÇö | Show intent table + 4D matrix. |\n| `/tp load` | ΓÇö | Load `reference/operating-manual.md` (deep reference). |\n| `/tp <artifact-path>` | Two-lens | Same as default but target is a file. |\n| `/tp horizon=now\\|next\\|later` | Two-lens or inline | Calibrates which domains fire. Auto-detected from phrasing when not explicit. |\n| `/tp confidence=high\\|medium\\|low` | Two-lens or inline | Calibrates depth to uncertainty. Auto-detected from hedging when not explicit. |\n\n### Horizon auto-detection\n\n| Horizon | Trigger phrasing | Effect |\n|---|---|---|\n| **now** | \"what should I do?\" / \"is this right?\" / \"should I ship?\" | Skip pre-mortem, steelman, second-order |\n| **next** | \"what should we do next?\" / \"what should we prepare for?\" | Include second-order; skip pre-mortem |\n| **later** | \"where is this going?\" / \"long-term\" / \"strategy\" | Full depth (all domains) |\n| **all** (default) | \"what do you think?\" / no timeframe signal | Full depth |\n\nState the inferred horizon in the synthesis header. When ambiguous,\ndefault to **all**. Horizon only REMOVES domains for time-pressured\nquestions ΓÇö it never reduces rigor below domains 1-4 + disconfirmation.\n\n## `/tp session` ΓÇö session-end opportunity review\n\n**Fires via semantic intent classification** when the user asks what they\nshould care about, what they're missing, or what matters from this session.\n**Invoked by `/close`** before emitting the final summary ΓÇö catches what\nthe close gates miss.\n\nWhen the user asks a semantically equivalent form of \"what should we do\nnow?\", \"what are we forgetting?\", \"what are we missing?\", or language with\nequivalent implied intent ΓÇö the question spans NOW through LATER. The\nresponse must include four explicit passes:\n\n### NOW\n- Immediate obligations, blockers, incomplete work\n- Verification and activation gaps\n- Decisions required before proceeding\n\n### NEXT\n- **Recurring friction** ΓÇö operational failures that repeated this session\n  (detected by the `friction_detector` and surfaced as continuation candidates)\n- Reliability improvements exposed by current work\n- Reusable helpers suggested by repeated local workarounds\n- Missing tests or observability\n- Workflow and skill improvements\n\n### LATER\n- Architectural opportunities, scaling limits\n- Strategic research questions\n- Cross-workstream combinations\n- Replacements for repeated local workarounds (e.g., shared command-construction\n  helper to eliminate nested-shell quoting failures permanently)\n\n### FILTER AND BLIND-SPOT REVIEW\nCheck whether opportunities were hidden by:\n- Overly narrow task scope (\"that's out of scope\" ΓÇö but is it really?)\n- Only examining unfinished work (completed work reveals opportunities too)\n- Only examining code changes (workflow friction, tooling, docs matter)\n- Confusing safe interim state with desired end state\n- Rejecting ideas because they are not immediately actionable\n- Failing to reconsider prior rejections after new evidence\n- Treating the first viable solution as optimal\n- Failing to combine related mechanisms\n\n**Each material finding receives:**\n- One horizon: `NOW` | `NEXT` | `LATER` | `MONITOR`\n- One proposed disposition: `DO_NOW` | `EXISTING_WORKSTREAM` | `NEW_HANDOFF`\n  | `RESEARCH` | `MONITOR_WITH_TRIGGER` | `REJECT_WITH_RATIONALE`\n  | `NEEDS_USER_DECISION`\n\nCluster related findings into the smallest coherent workstreams. Do not\ncreate a handoff for every idea ΓÇö one handoff per cluster is the default.\n\n**Exception:** if the user explicitly narrows to immediate actions only\n(\"what should I do right now?\"), the response may narrow to NOW when the\nnarrowing is explicit.\n\n## Default `/tp` ΓÇö the four-step process\n\nThe full subagent prompt template (Steps A-D, evidence tagging, output\nformat, disconfirmation gate) lives in **`protocol.md`**. SKILL.md is the\nrouting layer; protocol.md is the instrument.\n\n### Step 0.5: Critique-history surface + optional preflight\n\n**Critique history (mandatory, runs first):**\n```bash\npython P:/.data/tp_critique_log.py auto --limit 20\n```\nThe `auto` command infers outcomes from git history (commits after critique\nΓåÆ \"likely-acted-on\"; no commits ΓåÆ \"likely-ignored\") and displays patterns.\nInclude the patterns output at the top of the Step 3 synthesis. For ADHD\nmemory: the patterns ARE the external memory aid, appearing at the top of\nevery critique.\n\n**Preflight (when the question touches existing implementations):**\n\nRun a scoped `/preflight` when the question references an existing\ncapability, proposes changing/replacing something that might already exist,\ntouches architecture/hooks/skills/plugins/config, or the answer depends on\nwhat's already in the workspace vs. what's missing. Skip for pure reasoning,\nsession-work, or preference questions.\n\n```powershell\npython P:\\.agents\\skills\\preflight\\scripts\\discovery_audit.py `\n  --scope <relevant scope(s)> `\n  --target <capability or entrypoint tokens> `\n  --output P:/tmp/tp-preflight.json `\n  --fail-on-conflict\n```\n\nFold key findings into Step 1's context bundle as verified facts. Disclosure\nin synthesis header: \"Preflight: run (scoped to <scopes>)\" or \"skipped\n(pure reasoning question).\"\n\n*Why:* the 2026-07-22 exec-gate plugin was built without checking whether\nGrok already had permission-gating capabilities. A preflight check before\nthe /tp critique would have surfaced the native capabilities first.\n\n### Step 1: Extract context bundle (~500 tokens)\n\nThe bundle the fresh subagent receives (instead of the full conversation ΓÇö\npreserves the \"fresh lens\" property while giving load-bearing facts):\n\n- **User's stated goal** (one sentence)\n- **Verified facts** (each with a one-line receipt: file:line or tool-call ref)\n- **What was tried and rejected** (with reasons ΓÇö prevents re-proposing dead ends)\n- **Hard constraints** (explicit user boundaries)\n- **Session transcript path** when the critique depends on conversation history:\n  `~/.grok/sessions/<encoded-cwd>/<session-id>/chat_history.jsonl` ΓÇö include\n  so the subagent can grep it for specific decisions rather than reading\n  the entire 500K+ char file.\n\nKeep under ~500 tokens. If it grows past that, the question is too broad\nfor `/tp` ΓÇö surface that to the user instead of spawning on an\nunder-specified target.\n\n### Step 2: Spawn fresh subagent (from a model pool, not inherit-or-fail)\n\n**Session-state gate:** If quick-fit screening routed inline, skip to Step 4\nwith the inline disclosure.\n\n`/tp`'s two-lens value depends on the fresh subagent actually spawning.\nOriginal Step 2 spawned with `model` omitted and fell to inline on any\nerror ΓÇö structurally same-lens ~5 times in session 019f821c under Token\nPlan 429s. Fix (2026-07-22): try a **pool** of spawn_subagent-compatible\nmodels before degrading to inline.\n\n**The spawn_subagent-compatible pool** (verified 2026-07-22; subset of\nhost roster ΓÇö NOT full config.toml):\n\n| Try order | Slug | Lane | Cost | Latency |\n|-----------|------|------|------|---------|\n| 1 | `nvidia-nemotron-3-ultra` | Reasoning | **Free** | 7.5s |\n| 2 | `glm-5-2` | Reasoning | Subscription (rationed) | 8.0s |\n| 3 | `nvidia-inkling` | Reasoning | **Free** | 2.9s |\n| 4 | `go-mimo-v2-5` | Code | Paid OR | 5.2s |\n| 5 | parent-inherited | Reasoning | Paid | varies (weakest lens) |\n\nKnown-broken slugs excluded: dgemma (empty content), deepseek/kimi\n(serialization), qwen (401), mistral (422), ccr-ornith (serialization\nunder load).\n\n**Selection logic (pool, not chain):** try each slug in order; if spawn\nreturns content (not 429/401/serialization/empty), record which ran +\nwhich failed (for Step 3 disclosure) and break. If ALL fail, fall to\nStep 4 inline.\n\n`/tp --model <slug>` override: skip the pool, use that slug directly.\n\n**Tool access:** the `general-purpose` subagent has access to `read_file`,\n`grep`, `list_dir`, `run_terminal_command`, `web_search`, `web_fetch`,\n`spawn_subagent`, `search_tool`, `use_tool` (MCP). The subagent does NOT\n**inherit** the parent conversation. It **can** access session transcripts,\nworkspace files, and wiki via `read_file`/`grep` if the prompt includes\nthe path or instructs it to look. The orchestrator should provide the\ntranscript path in the bundle when the critique depends on conversation\nhistory, and instruct the subagent to grep it for specific terms.\n\n**Pass the subagent `protocol.md` explicitly.** The orchestrator must\nconstruct the spawn prompt with the absolute path and an explicit\n\"read-and-follow\" instruction ΓÇö a bare \"follow protocol.md\" reference\nwill not resolve. Use this spawn template:\n\n```\nRead and follow the file at C:/Users/brsth/.grok/skills/tp/protocol.md\nin full ΓÇö it contains the critical-friend protocol (Steps A-D, evidence-\nbasis tagging, output format, disconfirmation gate, self-rationalization\ncheck). Do not free-form the critique; follow protocol.md's structure.\n\nTarget: <the artifact, prior-turn summary, or described decision>\nHorizon: <now | next | later | all>\nConfidence: <high | medium | low>\nContext bundle:\n<the bundle from Step 1 ΓÇö verified facts, prior rejections, constraints,\n[transcript path if conversation-dependent]>\n```\n\nThe absolute path is mandatory because the subagent starts without the\nparent's CWD context. Relative paths like `protocol.md` or `skills/tp/\nprotocol.md` will not resolve from the subagent's working directory.\n\n### Step 3: Verification synthesis (the deliverable)\n\nWhen the fresh subagent returns, **do not propagate its findings unchecked.**\nRun the spot-check gate (per `~/.grok/AGENTS.md` \"Subagent synthesis ΓåÆ\nreport gate\").\n\n**Tool-use disclosure gate (mandatory):**\n- If subagent made **0 tool calls** AND target was a file/workspace object:\n  disclose \"ΓÜá∩╕Å Bundle-only critique: the fresh subagent made 0 tool calls.\n  Findings derive solely from the bundle I provided. Treat `[from-bundle]`\n  findings as provisional.\"\n- If findings predominantly `[from-bundle]` on a file target: weight lower,\n  flag the \"fresh lens\" was illusory.\n- If substantive tool calls with `[from-file-read]`/`[from-grep]`/`[from-command]`:\n  weight normally; lens is genuinely independent.\n\n**Disconfirmation-section gate (mandatory):**\n```bash\npython \"$env:USERPROFILE/.grok/skills/www/scripts/validate_disconfirmation.py\" --tp-subagent \"<subagent_output>\"\n```\nIf validator fails (exit 1), re-prompt or flag: \"ΓÜá∩╕Å Subagent skipped the\nmandatory Disconfirmation section.\"\n\n**Verdict-consistency gate (mandatory):**\n```bash\npython \"$env:USERPROFILE/.grok/skills/www/scripts/validate_verdict_consistency.py\" --tp-synthesis \"<synthesis_output>\"\n```\nCatches PROCEED declared while listing open gaps (closure-pressure\nminimization pattern, observed 2026-07-24).\n\n**Model-disclosure check (mandatory):** state which pool member ran +\nany that failed. Credit cross-family (nemotron/glm/mimo) as fresh-context\nAND fresh-model (strongest two-lens). If only parent-inherited worked:\n\"Fresh subagent: parent-inherited only ΓÇö fresh-context but same-model lens.\"\n\n**Per-finding checks:**\n1. **Verification:** does this cite a claim I can confirm/refute from session\n   evidence? If subagent claims \"X doesn't exist\" and I read X earlier,\n   flag it.\n2. **Novelty:** was this already considered and rejected? Mark\n   \"already-evaluated ΓÇö see <reference>\" rather than treating as new.\n3. **Integration:** does this change the recommendation, or real-but-immaterial?\n   Tag `changes-recommendation`, `material-but-not-blocking`, or `noted-but-immaterial`.\n\n**Mandatory disclosure on disagreement:** if synthesis disagrees with\nsubagent verdict (e.g. subagent says BLOCK, synthesis says PROCEED), state\nboth positions explicitly and explain. Do not silently override.\n\n**Critique logging (after synthesis delivered):**\n```bash\npython P:/.data/tp_critique_log.py append \\\n    --target \"<one-sentence target>\" \\\n    --verdict <PROCEED|REVISE|BLOCK> \\\n    --horizon <now|next|later|all> \\\n    --domains \"<comma-separated>\" \\\n    --findings \"<semicolon-separated>\" \\\n    --model <slug or \"inline-session-state\">\n```\n\n### Step 4: Inline fallback (when subagent spawn fails)\n\nIf `spawn_subagent` returns an error (429, quota, agent-type-not-found,\nimmediate exit), fall back to running the critique **inline**.\n\n**Mandatory disclosure:**\n```\nΓÜá∩╕Å INLINE FALLBACK: The fresh subagent could not be spawned\n(error: <one-line reason>). Running inline in the current agent's context.\nThis shares the conversation's framing anchor and is STRUCTURALLY WEAKER\nthan a two-lens critique. Treat output as provisional ΓÇö for high-stakes\ndecisions, retry when the blocker clears, switch models, or use /red-team.\n```\n\nThe inline critique follows Steps A-D of `protocol.md` but cannot claim\nthe \"another lens\" property. **Do not silently fall back** ΓÇö that presents\nsame-lens critique as two-lens, the exact failure mode Costa & Kallick\nwarn against.\n\n## Developer preferences (load before critiquing)\n\n- **Optimal long-term over minimal-diff** ΓÇö challenge both over-engineering\n  (gold-plating) AND under-engineering (cuts that will cost later).\n- **Transition effort is not a selection criterion** ΓÇö do not credit an\n  option for being smaller.\n- **Positive future ROI is the bar** ΓÇö the question is never \"is this\n  correct?\" but \"is this the best long-term answer?\"\n\nSource: `~/.grok/AGENTS.md` and `~/.claude/Claude.md` ┬º \"Implementation\nPrinciples.\"\n\n## `/tp help` ΓÇö intent categories and variants\n\nWhen invoked as `/tp help` / `/tp --help` / `/tp -h`, output this and stop:\n\n```\n/tp ΓÇö Critical-friend / thought-partner\n\nTwo-lens critique: a fresh subagent provides a different lens, then the same\nagent verifies and integrates findings against session evidence.\n\n  WHEN YOU WANT TO...                  USE\n  ΓöÇΓöÇΓöÇΓöÇΓöÇΓöÇΓöÇΓöÇΓöÇΓöÇΓöÇΓöÇΓöÇΓöÇΓöÇΓöÇΓöÇΓöÇΓöÇΓöÇΓöÇΓöÇΓöÇΓöÇΓöÇΓöÇΓöÇΓöÇΓöÇΓöÇΓöÇΓöÇΓöÇΓöÇΓöÇΓöÇ ΓöÇΓöÇΓöÇΓöÇΓöÇΓöÇΓöÇΓöÇΓöÇΓöÇΓöÇΓöÇΓöÇΓöÇΓöÇΓöÇΓöÇΓöÇΓöÇΓöÇΓöÇ\n  Challenge a decision or proposal     /tp  (default, two-lens)\n  Think through something quickly      /tp quick\n  Diagnose what went wrong             /tp check\n  Review what matters from a session   /tp session  ΓåÉ recommended\n  Focus on immediate next steps        /tp  (auto-detects \"now\")\n  Think about long-term direction      /tp  (auto-detects \"later\")\n  Critique a specific file             /tp <path>\n  See this help again                  /tp help\n\n  Default /tp: two-lens critique (~60-90s)\n  /tp quick:    same-agent dialogue (fast)\n  /tp session:  inline NOW/NEXT/LATER review\n                Also invoked by /close before final summary.\n\n  Horizon (now/next/later) is auto-detected from phrasing.\n  Confidence (high/medium/low) is auto-detected from hedging.\n  You don't need to type horizon=now ΓÇö just ask \"what should I do?\"\n\n  The skill logs each invocation. After 20+ runs, the patterns summary\n  shows which categories fire most and whether outcomes are acted on.\n\nUnder the hood ΓÇö each invocation is a point in a 4-dimensional space:\nLENS ├ù HORIZON ├ù TARGET ├ù POSTURE. The semantic classifier picks the\npoint automatically. Explicit invocation (e.g. /tp session) overrides.\n```\n\n## Related thinking tools (informational ΓÇö NOT prerequisites)\n\n> ΓÜá∩╕Å **The skills below are NOT dependencies.** `/tp` works fully without\n> them. They are listed here so a future reader understands where /tp's\n> ideas came from. **Do not** try to invoke `/reason`, `/skeptic`, `/tot`,\n> or `/sequential-thinking` before running `/tp` ΓÇö that is a\n> misreading of this section. The `cc-skills-thinking` plugin is disabled\n> on this host; these slugs will fail to resolve if invoked directly.\n\nThese are sibling skills in the `cc-skills-thinking` plugin. If the plugin\nis enabled, they compose with `/tp`; if disabled (current state), the\nconcepts still inform how `/tp` works internally.\n\n| Skill | What it does | Relationship to /tp |\n|---|---|---|\n| `/reason` | Unified reasoning engine routing by epistemic state (confidence) | `/tp`'s confidence dimension borrows from `/reason`'s confidence-routing table. `/reason` is deeper on pure reasoning; `/tp` is broader (framing, anchoring, solution-space). |\n| `/skeptic` | AI output validation using cognitive frameworks + evidence checking | `/tp`'s disconfirmation gate and evidence-basis tagging serve the same purpose. `/skeptic` is more focused on hallucination detection. |\n| `/tot` | Tree-of-thoughts ΓÇö explore multiple branches | `/tp`'s core domain 5 (solution-space broadening) is a lighter version. Use `/tot` when you need exhaustive branch exploration. |\n| `/sequential-thinking` | Generate-Critique-Improve loop | `/tp`'s two-lens architecture is one round; `/sequential-thinking` iterates. Future enhancement: optional GCI second round in `/tp` when verdict is REVISE/BLOCK. |\n\nThese are **cross-references, not dependencies.** `/tp` works fully without\nthe thinking plugin. If available, they offer deeper single-purpose\nreasoning; `/tp` remains the general-purpose critical-friend entry point.\n\n## Failure modes (vocabulary for `/tp check` and `/tp <mode>`)\n\nThese are **diagnostic vocabulary**, not the opening stance of default\n`/tp`. They apply when the user runs `/tp check`, `/tp <mode>`, or asks\nabout a specific failure pattern. The full table (9 modes) and circuit\nbreaker live in **`reference/failure-modes.md`**.\n\nSummary of the 9 modes: (1) agreeableness bias, (2) premature solutioning,\n(3) false precision, (4) solution vending, (5) silent reframe, (6)\nperformative rigor, (7) fabricated causal chain, (8) hidden decision\ndensity, (9) refactor dismissal.\n\n**Meta-guard:** if `/tp check` produces a generic \"I'll be more rigorous\"\nwithout naming a specific failure with evidence, that itself is mode 6.\n\n## Adjacent skills ΓÇö do not duplicate\n\n| Skill | Distinction |\n|---|---|\n| `/review` | Code/diff review with file:line findings. `/tp` is premise critique. |\n| `/red-team` | Adversarial multi-agent review with PROCEED/REVISE/BLOCK + disk-backed findings + telemetry. `/tp` is collaborative single-agent + synthesis, much lighter. |\n| `/debrief`, `/aar` | Session retrospective. `/tp` is live critique of a current question. |\n| `/design` | Design doc writer/reviewer loop. `/tp` is general-purpose critique; `/design` is domain-specific. |\n| `/preflight` | Evidence-backed discovery inventory. `/tp` Step 0.5 may invoke a scoped `/preflight`. |\n| `/brainstorming` | Full design-doc pipeline. `/tp` handles solution-space broadening itself via core domain 5. |\n\n## Boundaries\n\n- `/tp` does **not auto-fire.** The user invokes it.\n- `/tp` is **not a reprimand.** Matter-of-fact tone.\n- **`/tp` verdicts are advisory, not binding.** The operator decides. A\n  directive after the critique wins ΓÇö do not refuse to execute based on\n  the critique verdict.\n- `/tp quick` does not pretend to be default `/tp`. Same-lens limitation\n  is explicit.\n- Default `/tp` discloses inline fallback honestly.\n- **Deep references:** `protocol.md` (subagent prompt template),\n  `reference/operating-manual.md` (full reconstructed prompt + craft\n  exemplars), `reference/failure-modes.md` (diagnostic vocabulary +\n  circuit breaker). Load via `/tp load`.\n\n## Falsifier for this skill\n\n`/tp` is wrong if, within 6 months:\n\n- The fresh subagent consistently shares the orchestrator's framing\n  (the \"fresh lens\" is illusory ΓÇö spawn isn't different enough).\n- The fresh subagent consistently makes 0 tool calls and critiques only\n  from the bundle (observed 2026-07-21; structural fix = explicit tool-use\n  protocol in `protocol.md`).\n- The same-agent synthesis consistently rubber-stamps subagent findings\n  (verification gate captured ΓÇö becomes theater).\n- Default `/tp` is always invoked as `/tp quick` because latency is\n  unacceptable (two-lens cost not worth it).\n- **The skill spawns a subagent for a session-state question** (observed\n  2026-07-23: 188.7s / 14 tool calls wasted). If the carve-out is removed\n  and session-state spawns resume, this falsifier has triggered.\n\nIf any pattern appears, iterate this SKILL.md.\n\n## Provenance\n\n- Costa, A. L., & Kallick, B. (1993). \"Through the Lens of a Critical\n  Friend.\" *Educational Leadership*, 51(2), 49-51.\n- Two-lens architecture derived from 2026-07-20 session: `/critical-friend`\n  was built standalone, then rolled into `/tp` as default after recognizing\n  same-agent self-critique is structurally weaker than fresh-subagent\n  critique.\n- Solution-space broadening (core domain 5) from 2026-07-22: the operator\n  corrected that broadening is the critic's job, not a routing decision ΓÇö\n  you cannot evaluate whether X is optimal without knowing what Y and Z\n  look like.\n- Preflight grounding (Step 0.5) from 2026-07-22: exec-gate plugin built\n  without checking existing permission-gating capabilities.\n- Session-state inline carve-out from 2026-07-23: two cancelled spawns for\n  session-close questions; 188.7s wasted.\n- Horizon parameter from 2026-07-23 /www research: Now-Next-Later framework\n  (Intercom). Confidence dimension from `/reason` routing table\n  (2026-07-25 thinking-skills mining).\n- Verification + novelty + integration checks from `~/.grok/AGENTS.md`\n  \"Subagent synthesis ΓåÆ report gate\" (originally after the cc-council\n  incident).\n- 4D matrix rewrite (2026-07-25): the operator identified the 4D model\n  (lens ├ù horizon ├ù target ├ù posture) as the correct organizing principle,\n  cleaner than the accreted 13-variant list. See handoff\n  `P:/docs/handoffs/tp-rewrite-20260725/HANDOFF.md`.\n\r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\Program Files\\\\PowerShell\\\\7\\\\pwsh.exe\" -Command \"Get-Content -Raw -LiteralPath 'C:/Users/brsth/.claude/plugins/cache/local/cc-skills-sdlc/1.0.237/skills/__lib/evidence_tiers.md'\"","aggregated_output":"# Evidence Tiers and Quality\r\n\r\n## Evidence Tiers\r\nConfidence tags: `(Tier [0-4], [0-100]%)`\r\n\r\n| Tier | Ceiling | Sources |\r\n|------|---------|---------|\r\n| **Tier 1** | 95% | Execution artifacts, logs, test output, telemetry |\r\n| **Tier 2** | 85% | Official docs, specs, peer-reviewed reference |\r\n| **Tier 3** | 75% | Static analysis, logical derivation, symbols |\r\n| **Tier 4** | 50% | Comments, unverified claims, speculation |\r\n\r\n## Rules of Evidence\r\n- **Weakest Link**: Overall confidence cannot exceed the weakest tier in the causal chain.\r\n- **Mixed Tiers**: Ceiling = lowest tier used for a critical claim.\r\n- **No Evidence**: Maximum 50% confidence (Tier 4).\r\n- **Direct Observation**: Tier 1 requires tool output from the *current* turn/session.\r\n\r\n## Completeness Criteria\r\n- **Mechanism**: How it fails (log/trace).\r\n- **State**: What the system looked like (state file/DB).\r\n- **Outcome**: The final observable failure (transcript/output).\r\n- **Authority**: The registration/config (settings.json).\r\n\r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\Program Files\\\\PowerShell\\\\7\\\\pwsh.exe\" -Command \"Get-Content -Raw -LiteralPath 'C:/Users/brsth/.claude/plugins/cache/local/cc-skills-sdlc/1.0.237/skills/__lib/rca_investigation_protocol.md'\"","aggregated_output":"# RCA Investigation Protocol\r\n\r\n## Step -1: Surgical First Response (MANDATORY)\r\n**Do this IMMEDIATELY (no questions, no theater):**\r\n1. **Grep exact strings** from error message.\r\n2. **Read matching files** -- all of them.\r\n3. **State findings** immediately.\r\n\r\n## Step 0: Pre-Flight & Research\r\n- **Pre-Flight**: Check CKS/CHS for prior knowledge.\r\n- **Cognitive Stack**: Classify problem type (ERROR, PERFORMANCE, etc.) and select mental models.\r\n- **Internet Research**: Research official documentation before hypothesizing.\r\n\r\n## Step 1: Start with Falsifiable Symptom\r\n- Define what is wrong vs expected.\r\n- **Step 1.5: Telemetry Discovery**: Query `pretooluse_blocks.jsonl` and `diagnostics.db` first. Enumerate logs, state, and skill invocations.\r\n- **Step 1.6: Learned Patterns**: Check CKS for patterns from previous RCA sessions.\r\n- **Step 1.7: Multi-Angle Search**: Use symptom-type templates (PERFORMANCE, ERROR, INTEGRATION, INTERMITTENT, SECURITY).\r\n- **Step 1.8: Trace Execution Path**: MANDATORY for hangs/timeouts. Use logging to find exactly where it stops.\r\n- **Step 1.85: Runtime State Inspection**: Inspect live state if failure is silent.\r\n\r\n## Step 1.9: Hypothesis Generation (ToT)\r\n- Generate 3-7 hypotheses.\r\n- Score each: Reproducibility(0.3) x Recency(0.2) x Impact(0.5).\r\n- Prune Score < 0.3. Rank and test in order.\r\n\r\n## Step 2: Symbol-Level Trace\r\n- Use Serena MCP for data flow tracing (`find_referencing_symbols`).\r\n- **Step 2.5: First Divergence**: Identify the earliest mismatch point.\r\n\r\n## Step 3-9: Principles\r\n- Change one variable at a time.\r\n- Prefer instrumentation over intuition.\r\n- Minimize and reproduce.\r\n- Validate interfaces.\r\n- Fix structure before safeguards.\r\n- Verify on failure path.\r\n- Capture lesson in CKS.\r\n\r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\Program Files\\\\PowerShell\\\\7\\\\pwsh.exe\" -Command \"Get-Content -Raw -LiteralPath 'C:/Users/brsth/.grok/docs/file-editing-protocol.md'\"","aggregated_output":"# File editing protocol ΓÇö for all LLMs on this multi-agent Windows host\r\n\r\n> **Audience:** every LLM (Grok, Claude, Codex, and any other) that edits files on this host.\r\n> **Status:** final merged version. Synthesized from 5 review drafts + original.\r\n> **Type:** AGENTS.md content (behavioral rules, auto-loaded). NOT a skill file or wiki concept.\r\n> **Context:** this host runs multiple AI agents on a shared Windows filesystem. Session 2026-07-21 documented specific failures described in ┬º7.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 1. Tool selection\r\n\r\n### Cross-agent tool name mapping\r\n\r\nThis protocol uses generic operation names. Map to your host's tool:\r\n\r\n| Operation | Grok Build | Claude Code | Codex |\r\n|-----------|------------|-------------|-------|\r\n| **patch** | `search_replace` | `Edit` | `apply_patch` |\r\n| **write** | `write` | `Write` | (file write) |\r\n| **read** | `read_file` | `Read` | `read` |\r\n| **shell** | `run_terminal_command` | `Bash` | `bash` |\r\n\r\n### Selection table\r\n\r\n| Tool | When | Why |\r\n|------|------|-----|\r\n| **patch** | Default for any edit to an existing file | Targets a specific string; if the string changed (concurrent edit), the patch fails loudly instead of silently overwriting |\r\n| **read** (with `limit`) + **patch** | Most edits | Read the relevant section, then patch |\r\n| **shell** + Python atomic write | (a) 2+ sequential edits to same file, (b) patch has demonstrably failed, (c) multi-line replacement patch can't anchor | Single atomic operation; belt-and-suspenders on a host where silent failures have been observed |\r\n| **write** | New files only. **Never on existing files.** | Full-file replace. If another agent edited the file between your read and your write, their work is silently destroyed. |\r\n| `sed`/`awk` via shell | Use with caution on Windows | Available at `C:\\Program Files\\Git\\usr\\bin\\` but encoding handling is inconsistent. Test with the target file's encoding first. |\r\n\r\n### Why write is forbidden on existing files\r\n\r\nThe write tool replaces the entire file. It is fine for creating new files. On existing files, any concurrent edit between your read and your write is silently destroyed. Patch avoids this: if the target string is gone (because another agent changed it), the patch fails loudly instead of silently overwriting.\r\n\r\n### Encoding requirement\r\n\r\nAll file writes must specify `encoding='utf-8'` explicitly. Never rely on the platform default. On Windows, the default encoding is often `cp1252`, which silently corrupts non-ASCII characters (em-dashes, CJK, accented characters, box-drawing characters).\r\n\r\n### When to use Python atomic write (shell escape hatch)\r\n\r\nPython's `Path.write_text()` via shell is useful for:\r\n- **Batch edits** (2+ changes to the same file) ΓÇö write all changes in one script\r\n- **Edge cases** where patch fails to anchor\r\n- **Multi-file atomic operations**\r\n\r\nIt is NOT infallible ΓÇö session 2026-07-21 observed Python writes that were verified present but later vanished. It is better than patch for batch operations, but verification (┬º2) is still mandatory after using it.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 1b. Class C: shell quoting (third failure class, distinct from A/B)\r\n\r\nClass A = persistence (tool says success, disk empty). Class B = sequential\r\ncollision (one agent's `search_replace` reverts another's edit). **Class C is\r\nneither:** the model or harness emits shell syntax that does not match the\r\nexecuting shell. The command may exit 0 while corrupting data or never applying\r\nthe intended content. Atomic write does not help; the fix is idiom-matching and\r\ntemp files.\r\n\r\n### Symptoms\r\n\r\n- PowerShell `@'...'@` here-string passed inside the **Bash** tool ΓåÆ literal `@`\r\n  lines wrapped around the payload; exit 0. (claude-code#65162; fish-shell\r\n  sibling #71339.)\r\n- Large inline heredoc in `python -c \"...\"` with nested quotes ΓåÆ SyntaxError,\r\n  truncated payload, or silent wrong content.\r\n- Windows backslash path inside a bash double-quoted arg ΓåÆ escape-sequence\r\n  corruption.\r\n- `git commit -m \"multi\\nline\"` where the model assumed PowerShell here-string\r\n  semantics but the runner is bash.\r\n\r\n### Rule\r\n\r\nFor any **multi-line** or **nested-quote** shell payload, write it to a temp\r\nfile and invoke the tool against the file ΓÇö do not inline it in argv:\r\n\r\n| Need | Do | Don't |\r\n|------|----|-------|\r\n| Multi-line commit message | `git commit -F P:/tmp/msg.txt` | `git commit -m \"line1\\nline2\"` |\r\n| Multi-line PR/body | `gh pr edit --body-file P:/tmp/body.md` | `gh pr edit --body \"@'...'@\"` |\r\n| Python batch script | write `P:/tmp/x.py`, then `python P:/tmp/x.py` | `python -c \"import ...; '''nested'''...\"` |\r\n| PowerShell here-string | only when the runner is actually `pwsh` | inside the Bash tool, ever |\r\n\r\nMatch the quoting idiom to the **actual shell of the tool**, not the host's\r\ndefault shell. On a Windows dual-shell host, default multi-line payloads to a\r\nfile; never assume which shell a generic \"shell\" tool uses.\r\n\r\n### Source\r\n\r\n- [[external-silent-edit-and-shell-quoting-reports]] (wiki concept, 2026-07-21)\r\n- claude-code#65162, #62813, #71339; community bash auto-corrector gist\r\n\r\n### Optional mechanical mitigation (not in this protocol)\r\n\r\nA PreToolUse shell auto-corrector hook (rewrite Windows backslash paths,\r\n`$USERPROFILE`ΓåÆ`$HOME`, wrap bare cmdlets with `pwsh -NoProfile`, convert git\r\ncommit heredocs to `$'...'`) reduces Class C without relying on model memory.\r\nPattern: https://gist.github.com/kmgallahan/cf44f50293901c7d4034b21b52c5804f .\r\nTier 2 enforcement; separate plan.\r\n\r\n## 2. Verification\r\n\r\n### After a single edit:\r\n\r\n1. **Read the edited section back** ΓÇö confirm your change is present\r\n2. **Read a few lines before and after** ΓÇö confirm prior content survived\r\n3. **If your change is missing**: re-apply via Python atomic write\r\n4. **If prior content is missing** (concurrent edit collision): STOP. Recover before continuing (see Recovery below).\r\n5. **If patch fails with \"old_string not found\"**: another agent may have changed the file. **Re-read the file before retrying.** Do not retry the same old_string blindly.\r\n\r\n### After a batch of edits (2+ to the same file):\r\n\r\n1. Use Python atomic write from the start ΓÇö write ALL changes in one script\r\n2. Read back the full file once ΓÇö verify ALL your changes AND all prior content\r\n3. If anything is missing: restore and re-apply\r\n\r\n### Post-edit-chain regression diff (after 3+ edits to same file)\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit diff HEAD -- <edited-file> | head -80\r\n```\r\n\r\nScan for re-introduced patterns from earlier fixes. If the diff shows both additions and silent reversions of the same lines, a later edit regressed an earlier one.\r\n\r\n### Stale-read detection\r\n\r\nIf there have been **more than 5 tool calls** between your last read of a file and your edit to it, **re-read the file first.** Another agent may have changed it in the gap. This catches the \"read, think long, write on stale data\" failure mode.\r\n\r\n### Why read the surrounding lines, not just your edit\r\n\r\nReading only your changed line catches persistence failure but NOT overwrite of other content. The surrounding-lines read is the only way to detect that you clobbered another agent's work.\r\n\r\n### Recovery when prior content is missing\r\n\r\nThe recovery path depends on whether the file is git-tracked:\r\n\r\n- **Git-tracked file** (`P:/...` paths, anything in the workspace repo): restore via `git show HEAD:<path> > <path>`, then re-apply your change on top of the restored content.\r\n- **NOT git-tracked** (`~/.grok/skills/...`, `~/.claude/...`, etc. ΓÇö user config): no `git show` fallback. Recovery options:\r\n  1. Check for a recent backup (`.bak` file, tmp directory snapshot)\r\n  2. Check if a sibling agent session has a copy in their context window\r\n  3. If neither: report the loss to the user. **Do not invent content to fill the gap.**\r\n\r\nThis home-vs-workspace asymmetry is real ΓÇö recovery for user-config files is materially worse than for project files.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 3. Skill file locations (one location per skill, never duplicate)\r\n\r\nSkills exist in multiple scopes. When authoring or editing a skill, use ONE location:\r\n\r\n| Scope | Path | Purpose |\r\n|------|------|---------|\r\n| Bundled | `~/.grok/bundled/skills/<name>/SKILL.md` | Shipped with Grok. Don't edit (overwritten on reinstall). |\r\n| User | `~/.grok/skills/<name>/SKILL.md` | **Default for new skills and personalizations.** |\r\n| Workspace | `P:/.grok/skills/<name>/SKILL.md` | Project-specific skills only (skills that depend on `P:/packages/<x>/` code paths). NOT for Grok skills. |\r\n| Plugin | `P:/packages/.claude-marketplace/plugins/<p>/skills/<n>/SKILL.md` | From plugin installs. Don't edit directly. |\r\n\r\n### Rules:\r\n\r\n- **Never put the same skill in two scopes** ΓÇö they will diverge silently\r\n- **Before editing a skill, verify the path exists** ΓÇö if it doesn't, you have the wrong scope\r\n- **If unsure which scope a skill is in**, check both: `~/.grok/skills/<name>/` and `P:/.grok/skills/<name>/`\r\n- **Grok skills ALWAYS live at user scope** ΓÇö `/handoff`, `/tp`, `/plan`, `/go`, `/design`, `/review`, `/aar`, `/www`, `/wiki`, `/web` are Grok skills, not project skills. If you find yourself putting any of these at workspace scope, you have the wrong scope.\r\n- **When authoring a new skill**, default to user scope (`~/.grok/skills/`)\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 4. No destructive git\r\n\r\nThis is a multi-agent shared host with a remote (`origin` ΓåÆ `github.com/EndUser123/p.git`). Rewriting shared history is silent, irreversible, and undetectable until someone notices their work is gone.\r\n\r\n| Forbidden | Use instead | Why |\r\n|-----------|-------------|-----|\r\n| `git reset --hard` | `git reset --mixed` or `git stash` | Destroys uncommitted work from other agents |\r\n| `git push --force` / `--force-with-lease` | New commit on top that fixes forward | Overwrites commits other agents pushed between your fetch and push |\r\n| `git rebase -i` | Leave history as-is; fix forward | Rewrites all downstream commit hashes |\r\n| `git filter-branch` / `git filter-repo` | Never | Rewrites entire history; all clones break |\r\n| `git commit --amend` (on pushed or shared commits) | New fix-up commit | Rewrites commit hash; anyone with the old hash diverges |\r\n| `git checkout -- <path>` | Re-read the file, then re-edit | Discards uncommitted edits from other agents on the same file |\r\n| `git clean -fd` | `git clean -nd` first to preview | Deletes untracked files including freshly-written work |\r\n\r\n**The cost of non-destructive git is cluttered history. Accept it.** Forward-fixing commits are correct on a multi-agent host.\r\n\r\n**Exception for `git commit --amend`:** acceptable only when ALL of:\r\n1. The commit has NOT been pushed (`git log --oneline origin/main..HEAD` must show it)\r\n2. You are the sole writer (no other agent has a working copy of the branch)\r\n3. No other agent has referenced the commit by hash (in handoffs, wiki, logs)\r\n4. You are amending in the same tool-call sequence as the original commit\r\n\r\nWhen in doubt, fix forward.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 5. Cross-agent coordination\r\n\r\nThis host runs multiple AI agent environments:\r\n- **Grok Build** ΓÇö primary (`[VERIFIED]` 9 session directories)\r\n- **Claude Code** ΓÇö present (`[VERIFIED]` `~/.claude/` with 2027 items)\r\n- **Codex** ΓÇö present (`[VERIFIED]` `~/.codex/` with AGENTS.md)\r\n- **CCR fleet** ΓÇö local models accessible via ccr-ornith\r\n\r\n`[INFERENCE]` These agents likely run concurrently at times. All share the same filesystem.\r\n\r\n### Rules:\r\n\r\n- **Always read before editing** ΓÇö another agent may have changed the file\r\n- **Verify prior content survives after your edit**\r\n- **Prefer additive edits** ΓÇö adding a section is safer than modifying existing content\r\n- **If you find a file in an unexpected state** ΓÇö don't overwrite. Read, understand, integrate.\r\n\r\n### Shared resources (high-collision-risk files)\r\n\r\nFor these files, use Python atomic write for any edit, verify immediately. **Append-only files (never `search_replace`, never full overwrite):**\r\n\r\n- `~/.grok/AGENTS.md` ΓÇö all Grok sessions read\r\n- `P:/AGENTS.md` ΓÇö all agents in this workspace read\r\n- `~/.claude/Claude.md` ΓÇö all Claude sessions read\r\n- `P:/.claude/CLAUDE.md` ΓÇö workspace Claude compat\r\n- `~/.codex/AGENTS.md` ΓÇö all Codex sessions read\r\n- `P:/.data/wiki/log.md` ΓÇö **append-only hard rule**. Use `open(path, 'a', encoding='utf-8')`, PowerShell `Add-Content`, or `P:/.data/wiki/scripts/append_log.py`. No `search_replace`, no full overwrite, ever. To correct a prior entry, append a new dated entry; never rewrite. (Rationale: ADR-008 append-only design choice + the 2026-07-21 log-wipe incident in [[file-edit-failures-two-classes]].)\r\n- `P:/.data/wiki/concepts/` ΓÇö any `/wiki` or `/www` run writes here\r\n- `P:/docs/handoffs/` ΓÇö any session may create or update handoffs\r\n- `P:/docs/plans/` ΓÇö multi-session planning artifacts\r\n- `~/.grok/skills/` ΓÇö every user-skill SKILL.md\r\n- `~/.grok/tool-fallbacks.md` ΓÇö any session may update\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 6. Recommended enforcement (hooks)\r\n\r\nThese rules are advisory in AGENTS.md. The following 3 are worth mechanical enforcement:\r\n\r\n| Hook | Type | What it does |\r\n|------|------|-------------|\r\n| Block write on existing files | PreToolUse | Check if target file exists. If yes, block with stderr: \"Use search_replace or Python atomic write for existing files. See file-editing protocol.\" |\r\n| Block destructive git | PreToolUse | Match `git reset --hard`, `git push --force`, `git filter-branch`, `git filter-repo`, `git clean -fd`, `git checkout --`. Block with stderr explanation. |\r\n| Warn on stale read | PreToolUse | If the last `read_file` for this path is older than 5 tool calls, warn (not block): \"File may have changed since last read. Consider re-reading before editing.\" |\r\n\r\n**Don't enforce by hook** (overhead exceeds safety value):\r\n- All \"verify after edit\" rules ΓÇö too frequent\r\n- \"Prefer additive edits\" ΓÇö judgment call\r\n- Recovery procedures ΓÇö only invoked when something went wrong\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 7. Session 2026-07-21 evidence\r\n\r\nOnly file-editing incidents. Each row cites what is verified vs unknown.\r\n\r\n| Incident | What happened | Verified? | Root cause | Rule |\r\n|----------|---------------|-----------|------------|------|\r\n| Log entries lost | 13+ entries written via Python + read-back verified, later found absent | `[VERIFIED]` log.md had 0 entries for 2026-07-21 | **UNKNOWN.** Correct path, Python write, verification ΓÇö all present. Edits vanished anyway. | ┬º2 (verification) |\r\n| Handoff edits lost (path) | Audit subcommand edits went to `P:/.grok/skills/handoff/` (non-existent) | `[VERIFIED]` P:/.grok/skills/handoff/ does not exist; correct path is ~/.grok/skills/handoff/ | **VERIFIED:** path confusion ΓÇö wrong scope | ┬º3 (verify path) |\r\n| Handoff edits lost (reversion) | Workspace handoff/SKILL.md kept reverting to HEAD | `[VERIFIED]` reflog shows amend operations that rewrote commits | `[INFERENCE]` concurrent agent commits overwrote uncommitted edits | ┬º4 (no destructive git) |\r\n| AGENTS.md edits lost | 3 sections vanished after Python write + read-back verification | `[VERIFIED]` correct path (P:/), Python write succeeded, read-back confirmed, later absent | **UNKNOWN.** Same unexplained pattern as log.md. | ┬º2 (verification) |\r\n| Amend operations | 2 commits amended in shared history | `[VERIFIED]` reflog shows `commit (amend)` at HEAD@{1} and HEAD@{2} | **VERIFIED:** amend rewrites hashes. Impact on other clones: `[UNVERIFIED]`. | ┬º4 (no amend) |\r\n\r\n### What we cannot explain\r\n\r\nEdits to AGENTS.md and log.md were:\r\n1. Written via Python `Path.write_text()`\r\n2. Verified by reading the file back immediately after writing\r\n3. Later found completely absent\r\n\r\n**Root cause is UNKNOWN.** The `file-operations.md` rule says \"edits can silently fail to persist\" ΓÇö this describes the symptom, not the mechanism. Untested hypotheses: harness-level snapshot/revert, hook restoring files, write not flushing to disk before snapshot, concurrent session overwriting.\r\n\r\n**Recommendation:** commit critical edits to git immediately after verification. Git's object store is not subject to the same failure mode as the working tree.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 8. Implementation\r\n\r\n### Canonical source\r\n\r\n`~/.grok/AGENTS.md` is the canonical location. Other context files should be thin pointers:\r\n\r\n- `P:/AGENTS.md` ΓÇö add: \"See `~/.grok/AGENTS.md` ┬º file-editing-protocol for full rules\"\r\n- `~/.claude/Claude.md` ΓÇö add pointer or duplicate (Claude may not read `~/.grok/`)\r\n- `~/.codex/AGENTS.md` ΓÇö add pointer or duplicate (Codex may not read `~/.grok/`)\r\n\r\n### Not in the wiki\r\n\r\nThese are behavioral rules, not knowledge. A wiki concept documenting the session evidence and root cause analysis is appropriate as a companion artifact ΓÇö it answers \"why do these rules exist?\" ΓÇö but the rules themselves live in AGENTS.md.\r\n\r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\Program Files\\\\PowerShell\\\\7\\\\pwsh.exe\" -Command \"Get-Content -Raw -LiteralPath 'P:/.data/wiki/concepts/multidimensional-root-cause-analysis-ai-agent-failures.md'\"","aggregated_output":"---\ntitle: \"Multi-dimensional root cause analysis for AI agent failures: Ishikawa fan-out adapted for LLM reasoning errors\"\ncreated: 2026-07-25\nsource: session-019f94c9\ntags: [root-cause-analysis, ishikawa, fishbone, fault-diagnosis, multi-dimensional, five-whys, methodology, ai-agent-failure]\nsummary: >\n  When an AI agent makes a wrong diagnostic conclusion (e.g., \"hooks not\n  registered\" from incomplete evidence), single-cause root cause analysis\n  (Five Whys) misses contributing factors. The Ishikawa/fishbone method,\n  adapted from manufacturing and medical diagnosis, fans out across\n  dimensions (mechanical, measurement, behavioral, process, environmental)\n  and finds multiple contributing causes simultaneously. Applied to the\n  receipt-system diagnostic error of 2026-07-25: four dimensions\n  contributed (measurement bug, schema ambiguity, sampling bias, behavioral\n  narrative closure). No single \"why\" chain would have found all four.\n  Includes the adapted Ishikawa category table for AI agent failures, the\n  prompt-level technique for applying it, and when to use each method\n  (linear vs. fan-out).\nagent: grok\nhost: grok\ncognitive_load: 4\nverification: multi-source-verified\nsources:\n  - https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8520040/ (Webster 2021, cognitive biases in diagnosis)\n  - https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ishikawa_diagram (Ishikawa/fishbone reference)\n  - https://www.opm.gov/policy-data-oversight/human-capital-management/closing-skills-gaps/root-cause-analysis.pdf (OPM Ishikawa methodology)\nrelations:\n  - target: wiki/concepts/problem-first-systems-decomposition.md\n    type: complements ΓÇö decomposition before solutions; this is decomposition of causes before fixes\n  - target: wiki/concepts/reactive-pattern-matching-and-closure-pressure.md\n    type: applies-to ΓÇö the root cause methodology was applied to diagnose this pattern\n  - target: wiki/concepts/premature-closure-narrative-sufficiency-external-approaches.md\n    type: related ΓÇö both target the same failure class from different angles\n---\n\n# Multi-dimensional root cause analysis for AI agent failures\n\n## Decision context\n\n**Why this methodology was needed:** session 2026-07-25 produced a multi-dimensional failure (the agent wrongly concluded \"hooks not registered\" from incomplete evidence). The operator asked for the causal chain to root cause. A linear Five Whys analysis would have found ONE cause and stopped; the actual failure had four contributing dimensions. This page documents the fan-out methodology that found all four, adapted from the Ishikawa/fishbone tradition for AI agent failures.\n\n## The problem with single-cause analysis on multi-dimensional failures\n\n**Five Whys** assumes a linear causal chain: each \"why\" drills deeper into a single cause. This works when there IS one root cause. It fails when multiple independent factors contribute:\n\n```\nFive Whys on the receipt-system failure:\n\nWhy did the agent say \"hooks not registered\"?\nΓåÆ Because evaluation summaries showed hook_registration_status: not_registered\nWhy did the summaries show not_registered?\nΓåÆ Because the evaluation script checks the wrong registration path\nWhy does it check the wrong path?\nΓåÆ Because it was written for a different hook discovery mechanism\nSTOP. Root cause: evaluation script bug.\n\nBUT THIS MISSES:\n- The agent sampled subagent sessions (sampling bias)\n- Zero-completion looks identical to zero-activity (schema ambiguity)\n- The agent didn't check raw files (behavioral narrative closure)\n```\n\nThe linear chain found the evaluation script bug (dimension 1) and stopped. It missed three other contributing causes. Any fix based solely on the linear chain would leave three dimensions unaddressed.\n\n## Ishikawa/fishbone adapted for AI agent failures\n\nThe Ishikawa diagram (fishbone) from manufacturing quality control addresses this by fanning out across categories simultaneously. Instead of drilling down one path, you enumerate ALL plausible cause categories and investigate each.\n\n**Adapted category table for AI agent systems:**\n\n| Category | Question | AI-agent-specific examples |\n|----------|----------|---------------------------|\n| **Mechanical** (code/tool/config) | Is the tool actually working? Is the config correct? Is there a bug? | Hook not registered in JSON; script exits with error; config path wrong; API returns unexpected format |\n| **Measurement** (how we observe) | Is what we're measuring actually what we think? Is the metric derived correctly? | Evaluation script checks wrong path; metric conflates two states; counter increments on wrong event; unit mismatch |\n| **Behavioral** (model/agent reasoning) | Did the agent reason from evidence or from narrative? Did it verify before claiming? | Narrative closed before evidence checked; claim stated as fact without receipt; confirmation bias; premature closure |\n| **Process** (workflow/skill design) | Does the process have a verification gate at the right point? Does it allow skipping? | No gate for mid-session diagnostic claims; receipt rule is advisory not structural; verification targets wrong claim class |\n| **Environmental** (host/platform) | Is there a race condition, path mismatch, or platform-specific behavior? | Multi-agent transcript race; env vars not exported to shell; glob discovery vs. config-path check; Windows path encoding |\n\n**How to apply it:**\n\n1. State the observed failure in one sentence\n2. For each category, ask: \"could a problem in this dimension have contributed to the failure?\"\n3. For each \"yes,\" investigate that dimension independently\n4. Classify each found cause as structural (fixable in code/config) or behavioral (probabilistic mitigation only)\n5. Fix structural causes first (permanent); design probabilistic mitigations for behavioral causes (may decay)\n6. Name the falsifier: \"if this cause were the only cause, would fixing it prevent the failure from recurring?\" If no, there are other dimensions.\n\n## Worked example: the receipt-system diagnostic error (2026-07-25)\n\n**Observed failure:** the agent concluded \"receipt system hooks are not registered\" from evaluation summaries showing `completion_attempts: 0` and `hook_registration_status: not_registered`. The conclusion was wrong ΓÇö the hooks were already firing via `verification-receipts.json` and had produced 31 receipt files with real data.\n\n**Ishikawa fan-out:**\n\n| Dimension | Cause found | Structural or behavioral? | Fix |\n|-----------|------------|--------------------------|-----|\n| **Mechanical** | Evaluation script checks a config-path registration mechanism that doesn't match Grok Build's glob-based `*.json` discovery | Structural | Fix `receipt_shadow_evaluation.py` to check whether receipt files exist, not whether a config key matches |\n| **Measurement** | `hook_registration_status: not_registered` is derived from the wrong check; it reports registration status that doesn't reflect actual hook firing | Structural | Derive registration status from actual receipt-file existence or shadow-log activity |\n| **Schema** | `completion_attempts: 0` is ambiguous ΓÇö it means \"no completion claims evaluated\" but looks identical to \"hooks didn't fire at all\" | Structural | Add `shadow_entries_total` and `receipts_written_total` fields to distinguish no-claims from no-activity |\n| **Sampling** | The agent sampled 5 most-recent evaluation summaries, all from subagent sessions that naturally have zero completion claims (subagents don't make completion claims) | Behavioral + structural | Behavioral: agent should check parent session, not just most-recent. Structural: add session-type field to summaries |\n| **Behavioral** | The agent treated the plausible narrative (\"zeros ΓåÆ not registered\") as sufficient without checking raw evidence (one `ls` of the receipt directory) | Behavioral | Receipt rule extension: cover diagnostic claims about system state, not just causal claims about runtime behavior. Plus: diagnostic claim gate (see handoff) |\n\n**Key insight:** 4 of 5 causes are structural (fixable in code). Only the behavioral dimension is probabilistic. If the structural causes are fixed, the behavioral cause becomes much less likely to recur ΓÇö because the misleading signals that triggered it would no longer exist.\n\n## When to use linear (Five Whys) vs. fan-out (Ishikawa)\n\n| Method | When to use | When it fails |\n|--------|------------|--------------|\n| **Five Whys** (linear) | Single-cause failures; mechanical bugs; clear causal chain | Multi-dimensional failures; systemic issues; when multiple independent factors contribute |\n| **Ishikawa** (fan-out) | Multi-dimensional failures; systemic issues; when the failure could have mechanical + behavioral + process causes | Simple bugs where one cause is obvious; time pressure (fan-out takes longer) |\n\n**Rule of thumb:** if the first \"why\" produces a cause that feels like \"the answer,\" ask \"is this the ONLY cause?\" If you're not sure, switch from linear to fan-out. The cost of fan-out on a single-cause problem is ~5 extra minutes of analysis. The cost of linear-only on a multi-cause problem is missing causes that produce recurrence.\n\n## Connection to our existing root-cause infrastructure\n\nOur `/aar` skill (Phase 4) has a layered root-cause model:\n```\nOBSERVED_FAILURE ΓåÆ IMMEDIATE_TRIGGER ΓåÆ PROXIMATE_CAUSE ΓåÆ CONTRIBUTING_CONDITIONS ΓåÆ SYSTEMIC_REUSABLE_CAUSE\n```\n\nThis is the linear model. It's strong for tracing a single failure chain to depth. The Ishikawa fan-out is the complement: it's weak at depth but strong at breadth. Using both:\n1. **Ishikawa first** to find all contributing dimensions\n2. **Five Whys on each dimension** to trace each to its root\n\nThis gives both breadth (no dimension missed) and depth (each dimension traced to its structural root cause).\n\n## The prompt-level technique\n\nWhen the operator catches a diagnostic error, the most effective prompt is the Ishikawa fan-out in question form:\n\n> \"The evaluation summaries show all zeros. Before concluding the hooks aren't registered, verify across dimensions:\n> - **Mechanical:** are the hook scripts registered in the dispatch JSON?\n> - **Measurement:** is `hook_registration_status` derived from actual firing or from a config check?\n> - **Behavioral:** did you check the raw files, or just the summary?\n> - **Process:** does any verification gate target diagnostic claims made mid-session?\n> - **Environmental:** could a platform-specific discovery mechanism explain the discrepancy?\"\n\nThis forces the agent to investigate multiple dimensions simultaneously rather than locking onto the first plausible narrative. It's the medical diagnosis field's \"differential diagnosis\" applied to AI agent reasoning ΓÇö force the consideration of alternatives before commitment.\n\n## Falsifier\n\nThis methodology is wrong if, after applying it to 5+ multi-dimensional failures, the Ishikawa fan-out consistently finds only one real cause (the other dimensions are always false positives). In that case, the linear method is sufficient and the fan-out is overhead.\n\nIf the fan-out consistently finds ΓëÑ2 real contributing causes per failure, the methodology is validated.\n\n**Current evidence:** 1 application (receipt-system failure, 2026-07-25). Found 5 causes across 5 dimensions. 4 were structural. The methodology was validated for this case. Needs 4+ more applications before generalizing.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [Webster: Cognitive biases in diagnosis and decision making](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8520040/) (2021, 88 citations) ΓÇö premature closure in medical diagnosis; the #1 cognitive error\n- [Ishikawa diagram (Wikipedia)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ishikawa_diagram) ΓÇö the original fishbone method from manufacturing quality control\n- [OPM: Ishikawa Root Cause Analysis Methodology](https://www.opm.gov/policy-data-oversight/human-capital-management/closing-skills-gaps/root-cause-analysis.pdf) ΓÇö US Office of Personnel Management's guide; \"In cases of human error, people are rarely the true root cause. Seek the system, policy, or process that allowed the error to occur.\"\n- [Chattopadhyay: Cognitive Biases in Software Development](https://cacm.acm.org/research/cognitive-biases-in-software-development/) (CACM) ΓÇö confirmation bias mapping to software engineering\n\r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
